    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542536/service-designer-hybrid": {
      "title": "Service Designer (Hybrid)",
      "description": "Service Designer (Hybrid) Apply now Job no: 542536 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology, Strategy, Cyber Employment Type : Full-time (35 hours per week) Duration: Fixed-term contract till 30 June 2029 Remuneration: $153,933 - $163,338 + 17% super + leave loading Location : UNSW Kensington Campus – Hybrid work arrangement About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: UNSW is undertaking a major transformation of its Learning Management System (LMS) and the services, processes and user experiences that support teaching and learning across the University. As a Service Designer within the LMS Program, you will help shape how students, educators and professional staff interact with the LMS and related services. Reporting to the HCD/UX Manager, you will work across the Program to understand user needs, map current and future-state service experiences, and identify opportunities to improve the services, processes and interactions that support teaching and learning at UNSW. Working closely with Service Owners, business stakeholders, product teams, designers, engineers and vendors, you will translate insights into journey maps, service blueprints, service requirements and design artefacts that support program decision-making and delivery. You will help connect user needs with business objectives and technical implementation, translating research and insights into practical service requirements, service models and design recommendations that support effective decision-making and delivery outcomes. You will contribute to discovery activities, proof-of-concepts, prototypes and pilot initiatives to test usability, functionality, scalability and service viability. Working with engineering, UX/UI, architecture, cybersecurity and delivery teams, you will help ensure solutions meet user needs while considering operational requirements, service support models and long-term sustainability. This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to one of UNSW's largest digital transformation programs and help improve the LMS-enabled experiences of students, educators and staff across the University. Responsibilities Summary: Conduct user research and discovery activities to understand the needs, behaviours and pain points of students, educators, professional staff and support teams interacting with the LMS ecosystem. Support workshops, interviews, co-design sessions and stakeholder engagement activities to gather insights and inform service design outcomes. Develop service design artefacts including journey maps, service blueprints, personas, stakeholder maps and future-state service models. Co-design end-to-end technology-enabled service experiences across the LMS ecosystem and associated processes, consider user, business, technical and operational requirements. Translate research findings and user insights into practical recommendations, service requirements and design outputs that inform program and solution decisions. Work closely with Service Owners, business stakeholders, product teams and vendors to identify opportunities for service improvements and enhanced user experiences. Collaborate with engineering, UX/UI, architecture, cybersecurity and delivery teams to ensure service design outcomes are understood and reflected in solution design and implementation. Contribute to the development and evaluation of proof-of-concepts, prototypes and pilot activities to test usability, functionality, scalability and service viability. Assess and communicate service viability considerations, including user value, operational impacts, scalability and implementation readiness, to support information program decisions. Support the design of operational processes, service models and supporting practices required for successful implementation of LMS-related services. Consider service support, operational processes and transition requirements when designing future-state services and experiences to help ensure successful adoption and ongoing delivery. Analyse user feedback, service metrics, operational insights and research insights to identify opportunities for continuous improvement, measure service effectiveness and inform decision-making. Apply Human-Centred Design and Service Design methods and practices across the LMS Program to ensure solutions remain aligned to user and stakeholder needs. Create and maintain clear documentation and design artefacts that communicate insights, recommendations and service design outcomes to diverse stakeholder groups. Align with and actively demonstrate the UNSW Code of Conduct and Values. Identify and control psychosocial and physical health and safety risks associated with tasks, projects and activities within the area of responsibility. For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: 5+ years of experience in Service Design, Business Analysis, or a similar role. Experience applying service design practices within complex digital transformation, enterprise technology or large-scale change programs, ideally involving technology-enabled services or platforms. Strong understanding of Human-Centred Design methods, including user research, journey mapping, service blueprinting, workshop facilitation and co-design. Experience translating user needs, research findings and business requirements into practical service and experience improvements. Experience working collaboratively with business stakeholders, technical teams, vendors and delivery teams in complex environments. Strong communication, facilitation and stakeholder engagement skills, including the ability to build relationships across diverse stakeholder groups. Experience contributing to discovery activities, pilots, proof-of-concepts or iterative testing approaches that inform service and product decisions. Experience creating service design artefacts such as journey maps, service blueprints, personas, ecosystem maps and future-state service models. Understanding of operational service considerations, service support requirements and the factors that contribute to sustainable service delivery and continuous improvement. Understanding of technology delivery approaches and experience working in Agile, Scrum or iterative delivery environments. Familiarity with enterprise platforms, digital products or service ecosystems and the operational considerations required to support them. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills, with the ability to use qualitative and quantitative insights to inform decision-making. Ability to manage competing priorities and work effectively both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW's aims, objectives, values, relevant policies and health and safety responsibilities. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world.  Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) Mandatory Application Questions To be completed while completing and submitting the application form. Share a detailed example of how you uncovered the true user needs when people say one thing and do another? What Human-Centred Design prototypes, methods, and artefacts have you used most frequently (e.g. user research, journey mapping, service blueprinting, co-design workshops)? Describe how you test your designs with people who have differing abilities and backgrounds? How have you translated these user insights into requirements, design decisions or implemented solutions? What experience do you have working within large-scale digital transformation, enterprise technology or platform implementation programs? How to Apply: Submit your CV along with a cover letter addressing your suitability for the role via the application portal before 11:30pm on the 14 th September 2026. Get in Touch: For queries about the role reach out the LMS Program Manager, Timothy Hands on: timothy.hands@unsw.edu.au For queries regarding the recruitment process, reach out to the Talent Acquisition Partner, Ayla Kamal on: ayla.kamal@unsw.edu.au * Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 02 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 14 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542596/data-officer": {
      "title": "Data Officer",
      "description": "Data Officer Apply now Job no: 542596 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Clinical/Medical Research Data Officer - The Kirby Institute Employment Type: Full - time (35 hours a week) Duration: 1-year Fixed Term contract Remuneration : $108,021 base, plus 17% superannuation and leave loading Work rights required: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. Location: Kensington, Sydney (3 days on-site minimum) About The Kirby Institute: The Kirby Institute is a world-leading health research institute at UNSW Sydney. We work to eliminate infectious diseases, globally. Our specialisation is in developing health solutions for the most at-risk communities. Putting communities at the heart of our research, we develop tests, treatments, cures and prevention strategies that have the greatest chance of success. Details of the role Join a high-performing research environment where your expertise in clinical data management will directly support impactful health research. The Data Officer is responsible for maintaining high-quality clinical and research data, including data cleaning, query resolution, data mapping, database design, development, testing and ongoing maintenance. The role will work predominantly with REDCap to support clinical data collection and study management, including the integration and transformation of data received from multiple sources such as spreadsheets, laboratory systems and other databases. The position will also contribute to improving and streamlining data-management processes, including opportunities for automation, data transformation, reporting and integration between systems. This role would particularly suit someone who enjoys working at the intersection of clinical data management and data engineering—designing robust clinical databases while also improving how data is ingested, transformed, validated, integrated and reported. The role forms part of a larger team consisting of research coordinators, research assistants, trial managers, data analysts and investigators. The position reports to the Clinical Data Scientist and has no direct reports. Some key skills required: Graduate biomedical science qualifications, computer science or other medical qualifications including Nursing with relevant experience in the conduct of clinical research. Demonstrated experience in data management, data cleaning, data query resolution and data mapping. Experience with REDCap database design, build, and data management viewed favourably alongside experience with Microsoft Fabric. Proven knowledge of International Council for Harmonisation Good Clinical Research Practice Guidelines (ICH GCP) as governed by the International Council for Harmonisation. Collaborate with study site staff to ensure high-quality clinical data entry and cleaning, offering training and guidance in data entry and database use. Develop and update procedures, tools and resources which complement the procedures to govern data cleaning, database design, building, testing and maintenance of clinical study databases. Build clinical study databases for the collection and analysis of clinical study data, including laboratory and questionnaire data, incorporating data validation rules to ensure consistency of data entry. Proven initiative, analytical and problem-solving skills, capacity to meet deadlines and ability to pay close attention to detail. Please refer to the position description for full details. UNSW Benefits and Culture 17% Superannuation and leave loading​ Flexible working ​ Additional 3 days of leave over the Christmas Period​ Access to lifelong learning and career development ​ Progressive HR practices​ More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here. Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. How to apply: Please click on Apply now to apply online. Applications should not be sent to the contact listed below. Please provide a resume and a separate document addressing the skills and experience listed in the Position Description. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW . Contact Frank McHenry, Talent Acquisition Associate E: f.mchenry@unsw.edu.au Applications close: Wednesday 16 th September before 11.30pm Sydney time UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. #LI-FM1 Position Description Advertised: 02 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 16 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542629/research-officer-the-kirby-institute": {
      "title": "Research Officer - The Kirby Institute",
      "description": "Research Officer - The Kirby Institute Apply now Job no: 542629 Work type: Part Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Clinical/Medical Research Research Officer - The Kirby Institute Employment Type: Part - time (14-21 hours a week) Duration: Fixed Term contract until 31 December 2027 Remuneration : $108,021 base pro rata, plus 17% superannuation and leave loading Work rights required: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. Location: On-Site, Kensington, Sydney About The Kirby Institute: The Kirby Institute is a world-leading health research institute at UNSW Sydney. We work to eliminate infectious diseases, globally. Our specialisation is in developing health solutions for the most at-risk communities. Putting communities at the heart of our research, we develop tests, treatments, cures and prevention strategies that have the greatest chance of success. Details of the role The Research Officer will provide essential laboratory support for surveillance and research activities focused on multiple neglected tropical diseases (NTDs) and other infectious diseases. The role involves conducting laboratory experiments and contributing to research projects that assess the burden of NTDs in endemic settings, evaluate the effectiveness of disease control and elimination strategies, and generate high-quality data to inform public health policy and practice. This is an excellent opportunity to contribute to impactful global health research within a collaborative and multidisciplinary team. You will apply a range of laboratory, analytical and project coordination skills to support serosurveillance and infectious disease research, generating high-quality data that informs disease control and elimination strategies. Working with researchers, collaborators and stakeholders, you will play a key role in laboratory operations, data management, reporting and study delivery while helping advance evidence-based public health outcomes. The role of Research Officer reports to a Post-Doctoral Fellow for all laboratory related work and has no direct reports. Some key skills required: A university degree in infectious diseases or epidemiology or related discipline, or an equivalent level of knowledge gained through a combination of education, training or experience. Experience in molecular and cell biology techniques relevant to infectious disease research, including quantitative qPCR, ELISAs, multi-bead serological assays, and microscopy. Perform serological analyses of dried blood spot samples using multiplex bead-based assays (Luminex platform). Assist with molecular and immunological laboratory procedures, including qPCR, ELISAs, microscopy, and related assays. Experience working with a range of computer systems and applications, including clinical or research sample inventories and laboratory databases, including platforms such as mySample, and analysis programs such as STATA, GraphPad Prism, Microsoft Excel. Knowledge of research protocols, experimental design and planning as involved in developing research projects and reporting against milestones. Manage the technical aspects of databases, including management of biological sample inventories, ensuring accurate tracking, storage, and ensuring research integrity in research databases. Demonstrated superior interpersonal communication skills to initiate and maintain effective stakeholder relationships whilst exercising discretion and confidentiality. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively and productively within a team, but also to take initiative and work independently while managing competing demands. Please refer to the position description for full details. UNSW Benefits and Culture 17% Superannuation and leave loading​ Flexible working ​ Additional 3 days of leave over the Christmas Period​ Access to lifelong learning and career development ​ Progressive HR practices​ More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here. Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. How to apply: Please click on Apply now to apply online. Applications should not be sent to the contact listed below. Please provide a resume and a separate document addressing the skills and experience listed in the Position Description. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW . Contact Frank McHenry, Talent Acquisition Associate E: f.mchenry@unsw.edu.au Applications close: Wednesday 16 th September before 11.30pm Sydney time UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. #LI-FM1 Position Description Advertised: 02 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 16 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542742/postdoctoral-fellow": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Fellow Apply now Job no: 542742 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate, Associate Lecturer Post-Doctoral Fellow Employment Type: Full - time (35 hours a week) Duration: 12 Month Fixed Term contract Remuneration : $118,467-$126,711 base, plus 17% superannuation and leave loading Work rights required: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. Location: On-Site, Kensington, Sydney The School of Biomedical Sciences As one of the largest teaching and research-intensive schools at UNSW, the School of Biomedical Sciences is uniquely placed to not only inspire the next generation of science and medical graduates but also lead in the discovery and development of tomorrow’s diagnostics, treatments and cures. It plays a key role in arming future medical practitioners, biomedical scientists and a broader community of health professionals, each of whom bring specialised knowledge and a diverse skill set to the frontiers of medical science. The school holds strong cross-faculty connections with biotechnology, chemistry, maths, physics and engineering; along with academic staff and key partners whose work is based in the clinical or biotech space, this creates a naturally synergistic and innovative environment. Details of the role A Post-Doctoral Fellow (Level A) is expected to contribute to the research effort of UNSW by undertaking laboratory-based and translational cancer research, developing research expertise through defined projects, and supporting the translation of discoveries into clinical applications. The position will contribute to a research project focused on developing nanomedicine-based RNA therapies for pancreatic cancer and lung cancer preclinical models. The role will involve molecular and cellular biology, synthesising nanoparticles, biomarker discovery, target validation, therapeutic assessment in preclinical cell culture models and animal models, and the design, execution, analysis and interpretation of preclinical and translational cancer studies. As an early-career researcher, the Post-Doctoral Fellow will be expected to maintain high-quality experimental records, contribute to research outputs and funding-related activities, and build collaborative relationships with clinicians, consumers, researchers and industry partners to support the translation of research findings. The Post-Doctoral Fellow reports to Professor Phoebe Phillips the Head of the Pancreatic Cancer Translational Research Group whose research program spans from cell biology discoveries, preclinical development of new therapeutics all the way to a current Phase-2 clinical trial and embedding correlative science into our precision medicine trials program and has no direct reports. Some key skills required: A PhD in a related discipline, and/or relevant work experience. Demonstrated ability to undertake high-quality academic research and conduct independent research with limited supervision. Contribute independently or as a team member in collaborative research with a focus to enhance the quality of research outcomes in the discipline area. Conduct research (as per the norms of the discipline) and/or enable research teams to create scholarly output that is recognised by peers. Undertake specific research project/s under the guidance of a research leader and contribute to development of research activities. Demonstrated track record of publications and conference presentations relative to opportunity. Contribute to development of competitive grant applications. Demonstrated ability to work in a team, collaborate across disciplines and build effective relationships. Please refer to the position description for full details. UNSW Benefits and Culture 17% Superannuation and leave loading​ Flexible working ​ Additional 3 days of leave over the Christmas Period​ Access to lifelong learning and career development ​ Progressive HR practices​ More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here. Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. How to apply: Please click on Apply now to apply online. Applications should not be sent to the contact listed below. Please provide a resume and a separate document addressing the skills and experience listed in the Position Description. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW . Contact Frank McHenry, Talent Acquisition Associate E: f.mchenry@unsw.edu.au Applications close: Wednesday 16 th September before 11.30pm Sydney time UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. #LI-FM1 (#LI-DNI) Position Description Advertised: 02 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 16 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542784/senior-lecturer-education-focused-skills-in-science": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer - Education Focused - Skills in Science",
      "description": "Senior Lecturer - Education Focused - Skills in Science Apply now Job no: 542784 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Senior Lecturer One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. THIS ROLE IS LOCATED IN SYDNEY, AUSTRALIA. The School of Biological, Earth and Environmental Sciences is a large, collaborative and multi-disciplinary school in the Faculty of Science at the University of New South Wales with over 170 academic, research and professional staff. In 2025, the school obtained $22M in research funding, produced over 800 scientific publications and is frequently rated among the top schools across UNSW in student experience surveys. The School incorporates a broad range of disciplines, enabling comprehensive education and research in the broad fields of Earth Science, Environmental Science, Physical Geography, Climate Science, Evolutionary Biology, Ecology and Marine Science and Oceanography. UNSW’s Education Focussed (EF) roles enable academics to specialise in education and devote their time to delivering high-quality teaching and pursuing initiatives to enhance the educational experience of our students. Those who are appointed to this prestigious specialisation drive educational excellence within the university’s teaching and learning communities. The Senior Lecturer in the School of Biological, Earth and Environmental Sciences conducts high-quality teaching in interdisciplinary, skills-focused science courses, leading the team that delivers the capstone subjects SCIF1000 Skills in Science and SCIF1001 Advancing Science. About the role Level C ($161k - $185k) plus 17% Superannuation and annual leave loading Continuing Full-time (35 hours per week) Specific responsibilities for this role include: Leadership of the team delivering SCIF1000 and SCIF1001 including course convening, and collaboration with academics delivering other Science Faculty (SCIF) courses. Apply a well-defined teaching philosophy and a critically reflective teaching practice that inspires student learning. Conduct classes, assess student learning achievements and support students in undergraduate and/or postgraduate courses in line with UNSW policy. Design and develop learning activities and resources, and assessment and feedback at both course and program level using a range of suitable approaches and learning environments. Support learning progression with students as individuals (through such activities as one-to-one consultation and advice on Educational Learning Plans (ELPs)) and as a cohort, and implement inclusive teaching practices aligned with Equity, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) principles) to achieve positive learning and employability outcomes for students. Manage course administration as Course Authority, including academic quality assurance. Make a positive contribution by leading School meetings and seminars and be a member of School/Faculty committees as required. Work collaboratively with peers across the UNSW in all aspects of academic endeavour, develop learning and teaching strategies and resources and contribute to an exchange of teaching ideas, and mentoring of staff. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values. Cooperate with all health & safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the psychosocial or physical health and safety of yourself or others. About the successful applicant (Selection Criteria) To be successful in this role you will have: A PhD in a biological, earth or environmental science discipline, and/or relevant work experience. Demonstrated experience in teaching and learning design using a range of pedagogical approaches, development and delivery of courses and programs at undergraduate and/or postgraduate level. Experience using, implementing and/or designing with educational technologies and online delivery methods. Evidence of teaching effectiveness and passion for educational excellence and fostering this in others. Evidence of professional development of teaching practice in self and others with the ability to advance and lead an inclusive culture of excellence in learning and teaching. Demonstrated success in initiating curriculum development and improvement. Evidence of highly developed interpersonal and organisational skills. Evidence of ability to support and inspire students from diverse backgrounds and support student equity, diversity and inclusion initiatives. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. You should systematically address the selection criteria listed within the position description in your application. Please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed. Contact: Alistair Poore E: a.poore@unsw.edu.au Applications close: September 30 th , 2026 Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au UNSW aspires to be the exemplar Australian university and employer of choice for people from diverse backgrounds. UNSW aims to ensure equality in recruitment, development, retention and promotion of staff and that no-one is disadvantaged on the basis of their gender, cultural background, disability, sexual orientation or identity or Indigenous heritage. We encourage everyone who meets the selection criteria to apply. UNSW partners with Australia’s leading diversity organisations, networks, and campaigns. Please refer to UNSW’s diversity offerings for further information on our flexible work and leave options, and support for carers (childcare, parent rooms, parental leave). Position Description Advertised: 02 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 30 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542803/associate-director-research-programs": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Research Programs",
      "description": "Associate Director, Research Programs Apply now Job no: 542803 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Professional Leadership, Research Administration support Employment Type: Full time Duration: Continuing position Remuneration : Senior Appointment Why your role matters As Associate Director, Research Programs, you will provide strategic and operational leadership for UNSW’s institutional research funding capability. Leading portfolios spanning researcher pathways, collaborative research development, and funding operations and compliance, you will connect these areas to strengthen UNSW’s success in securing competitive research funding. Working across Faculties, Research Grants & Contracts, Industry & Innovation and external partners, you will translate institutional priorities into coordinated funding outcomes and provide expert advice to senior leaders on funding strategy, research capability, performance, governance and emerging opportunities. This role reports to the Director, Research Strategy Office, and you’ll lead a team of 11 including 4 direct reports. About UNSW UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. We are a large organisation with a diverse and highly talented community doing extraordinary work. We are driven to be thoughtful, practical and purposeful in all we do, and this collective approach is what gives our work impact. It is also why UNSW is consistently ranked among the world’s top 20 universities in the QS World University Rankings and is a proud member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you’re seeking a career where you can thrive, be challenged and contribute to meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Hybrid/flexible working arrangements and are committed to supporting work-life balance while delivering meaningful outcomes. 17% superannuation contributions, annual leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice option Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Affordable on-campus parking About you: You will bring a relevant postgraduate qualification and/or extensive experience in a major research or government portfolio, together with a strong track record of developing and stewarding collaborative research partnerships. You can also demonstrate: Expert knowledge of the national and international research environment, including competitive funding sources, funding schemes, policies and regulatory requirements. Strong strategic, analytical and problem-solving skills, with the ability to use data to inform decisions and advise on complex issues. Demonstrated people leadership experience, including the ability to lead, develop and motivate high-performing teams, build capability, clarify expectations and foster a collaborative, inclusive and accountable culture. Highly developed project management, prioritisation and organisational skills. A proven ability to improve workflows, service delivery and quality through innovative solutions. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with strong attention to detail. Highly developed influencing, negotiation and consultation skills, including the judgement and discretion to manage sensitive matters. A collaborative leadership style and the ability to build credibility and effective relationships at all levels. A commitment to UNSW’s values, policies, guidelines and health and safety responsibilities. How to apply: Please submit your CV and a Cover Letter outlining your interest in the role and how your skills and experience align with the position requirements before 11.55pm, Sunday 20 th September 2026. A copy of the Position Description is available via Jobs@UNSW. Pre Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact: Shiree Thomas – Talent Acquisition Partner: shiree.thomas@unsw.edu.au Please note applications sent directly to the contact person will not be accepted NSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. #LI-ST1 Position Description Advertised: 02 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 20 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542549/research-associate-lastmile-logistics": {
      "title": "Research Associate – last-mile logistics",
      "description": "Research Associate – last-mile logistics Apply now Job no: 542549 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate The Opportunity: The appointee will work closely with researchers and industry practitioners from UNSW and Adiona Tech, as well as other industry partners, to develop and validate AI-powered methods for sustainable freight and last-mile logistics as part of a CRC-P project. The research will focus on optimising last-mile delivery operations, reducing emissions, and accelerating fleet electrification. A key objective is to translate an existing research prototype into a commercial-scale platform capable of supporting real-world logistics networks across Australia. The position provides a distinctive opportunity to undertake high-quality academic research while working directly with industry datasets, operational systems, and commercial software development. This is a unique industry postdoctoral position comprising a 0.8 FTE appointment through UNSW and a complementary 0.2 FTE appointment through Adiona Tech. Salary, Level A - $95,036 - $126,711 per annum + 17% superannuation (pro rata) Part Time: 28 hours (0.8 FTE) and 0.2 FTE appointment through Adiona Tech. Fixed-term contract: 12 months (potential for extension) Location: Kensington – Sydney, Australia (on campus attendance 3 days a week) About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. But what makes us different isn’t only what we do, it’s how we do it. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. The Faculty of Engineering at UNSW continues to be a national and international leader in engineering research and education, cementing its status as the University’s premier research faculty. As the largest Engineering Faculty in Australia, it graduates the highest number of engineers and maintains the largest research expenditure in the country. Through excellence in both fundamental and applied research, the Faculty strives to sustain and further its global impact, aiming to be ranked among the top engineering faculties worldwide. For more information, visit the Faculty website: https://www.unsw.edu.au/engineering/about-us Skills & Experience: A PhD in a related discipline, and/or relevant work experience. Relevant disciplines may include transport engineering, computer science, artificial intelligence, data science, machine learning, operations research, mathematics, statistics, or another quantitative discipline. Proven commitment to proactively keeping up to date with discipline knowledge and developments. Demonstrated ability to undertake high-quality academic research and conduct independent research with limited supervision. Demonstrated track record of publications and conference presentations relative to opportunity. Demonstrated expertise in one or more of the following areas: mathematical optimisation, vehicle routing and scheduling, machine learning, reinforcement learning, logistics simulation, predictive analytics, or transport modelling. Strong programming skills in Python and experience developing, implementing, and testing computational models or research software. Experience working with large and complex datasets and applying appropriate data management, analysis, and validation methods. Demonstrated ability to translate research concepts and computational methods into practical solutions suitable for real-world implementation. Experience with high-performance computing, cloud computing, software development, or commercial technology platforms would be highly regarded. Knowledge of electric vehicle operations, energy and charging models, emissions estimation, freight transport, or logistics decarbonisation would be desirable. Demonstrated ability to work in a team, collaborate across disciplines and build effective relationships. Demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively with industry partners and communicate technical concepts to both specialist and non-specialist audiences. Evidence of highly developed interpersonal skills. Demonstrated ability to communicate and interact with a diverse range of stakeholders and students. Ability to work effectively across university and industry environments and manage competing research, technical, and project priorities. Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Additional details about the specific responsibilities for these positions can be found in the position description. This is available via JOBS@UNSW . To Apply: Please click the apply now button and submit your CV, Cover Letter and Responses to the Skills and Experience. You should systematically address the Skills and Experience listed within the position description in your application. Please note: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Please note applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed below. Contact: Allyssar Hamoud – Talent Acquisition Associate E: a.hamoud@unsw.edu.au Applications close: 11:55 pm (Sydney time) on Tuesday 22 nd September 2026 UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 01 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 22 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542564/senior-research-associate-school-of-civil-environmental-engineering": {
      "title": "Senior Research Associate – School of Civil & Environmental Engineering",
      "description": "Senior Research Associate – School of Civil & Environmental Engineering Apply now Job no: 542564 Work type: Part Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate The Opportunity: A Senior Research Associate (Level B) is expected to carry out independent and/or team research within the field in which they are appointed and to carry out activities to develop their research expertise relevant to their particular field of research. The role of Senior Research Associate reports to Associate Professor Wengui Li and has no direct reports. Salary, Level B - $137,821 per annum + 17% superannuation (pro rata) Part Time: 7 hours Fixed-term contract: 12 months Location: Kensington – Sydney, Australia (on campus attendance 3 days a week) About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. But what makes us different isn’t only what we do, it’s how we do it. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. The Faculty of Engineering at UNSW continues to be a national and international leader in engineering research and education, cementing its status as the University’s premier research faculty. As the largest Engineering Faculty in Australia, it graduates the highest number of engineers and maintains the largest research expenditure in the country. Through excellence in both fundamental and applied research, the Faculty strives to sustain and further its global impact, aiming to be ranked among the top engineering faculties worldwide. For more information, visit the Faculty website: https://www.unsw.edu.au/engineering/about-us Skills & Experience: A PhD in a related discipline, and/or relevant work experience. Proven commitment to proactively keeping up to date with discipline knowledge and developments. Demonstrated track record in research with outcomes of high quality and high impact with clear evidence of the desire and ability to continually achieve research excellence as well as the capacity for research leadership. A track record of significant involvement with the profession and/or industry. High level communication skills and ability to network effectively and interact with a diverse range of students and staff. Demonstrated ability to work in a team, collaborate across disciplines and build effective relationships. Evidence of highly developed interpersonal and organisational skills. Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Additional details about the specific responsibilities for these positions can be found in the position description. This is available via JOBS@UNSW. To Apply: Please click the apply now button and submit your CV, Cover Letter and Responses to the Skills and Experience. You should systematically address the Skills and Experience listed within the position description in your application. Please note: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Please note applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed below. Contact: Allyssar Hamoud – Talent Acquisition Associate E: a.hamoud@unsw.edu.au Applications close: 11:55 pm (Sydney time) on Tuesday 8 th September 2026 UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 01 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 08 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/541173/senior-platform-engineer": {
      "title": "Senior Platform Engineer",
      "description": "Senior Platform Engineer Apply now Job no: 541173 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology Full time continuing role as a Senior Platform Engineer – Informatica Cloud Starting Salary $153,933 plus generous superannuation and leave loading Kensington, Sydney location, 2-3 days in the office, Hybrid working About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. It’s the reason we’re one of the top 20 universities in the world (QS top 20) and a member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. The Senior Platform Engineer – Informatica Cloud plays a strategic role in shaping, evolving and operationalising UNSW’s Master Data Management and enterprise data integration capability using associated cloud infrastructure and integration services in Azure. Working across MDM, architecture, cybersecurity, cloud, integration and business teams, the role translates complex platform and data challenges into robust, supportable and value-driven solutions. It leads platform improvement, onboarding patterns, automation, monitoring, DevSecOps practices and operational governance to enable reliable service delivery and future growth. The role also acts as a trusted advisor, uplifting capability across teams, promoting reusable engineering patterns, and ensuring platform decisions balance business value, security, data governance, operational risk and long-term enterprise sustainability for UNSW’s emerging data management needs and responsible AI-enabled service innovation at scale agenda. The role reports to Lead Solution Architect - MDM and Data and may have direct reports. Specific accountabilities for this role include: Provide senior technical leadership for the design, operation and continuous evolution of UNSW’s Master Data Management platform, Customer 360 capability and associated Informatica IDMC services. Shape the platform strategy and technical roadmap for Informatica Cloud, ensuring alignment with UNSW’s data management, digital transformation, integration, automation and responsible AI priorities. Translate complex and ambiguous business, data and integration requirements into clear platform approaches, technical patterns and secure, scalable, value-driven solutions. Lead the design and implementation of reliable, supportable and enterprise-ready platform services across Informatica IDMC/IICS, Azure, AWS and related integration environments. Oversee the onboarding of new data domains, applications, integrations, users and business services onto the MDM platform, ensuring appropriate architectural, security, operational and governance controls are embedded from the outset. Apply systems thinking to analyse data flows, platform dependencies, integration points, APIs, cloud services, security constraints and operational risks across UNSW’s technology ecosystem. Establish and uplift reusable platform patterns, standards, runbooks, automation scripts, monitoring frameworks and operational playbooks that improve consistency, resilience and delivery efficiency. Lead platform reliability, availability and performance management, including monitoring of platform health, job execution, integration performance, runtime environments, service availability, alerts, logs and operational events. Drive continuous improvement of platform operations through automation of repeatable administration, monitoring, deployment, alerting, incident response and service management processes. Support and mature DevSecOps practices for the MDM and data integration platform, including environment management, deployment controls, CI/CD alignment, infrastructure-as-code practices, security controls and operational handover. Ensure the MDM platform and related integration services comply with UNSW Technology standards for cybersecurity, privacy, identity and access management, data governance, logging, monitoring, resilience and service support. Provide trusted technical advice to the Head of MDM, architects, cybersecurity, cloud, integration, application and business teams on platform design, service onboarding, operational readiness and sustainable support models. Collaborate with business and technology stakeholders to clarify needs, assess feasibility, balance risk and value, and shape platform solutions that support current and future institutional data management needs. Lead technical troubleshooting, root cause analysis, remediation and escalation for complex incidents affecting Informatica Cloud, Customer 360, Azure services, AWS connectivity and related integration services. Evaluate emerging Informatica, cloud, data management, integration, automation and AI-adjacent capabilities, assessing their relevance to UNSW and recommending responsible adoption pathways. Promote strong data management practices across master data, data quality, metadata, lineage, integration design, secure data access and operational governance. Mentor team members and peers, sharing knowledge, technical patterns and platform engineering practices that uplift capability across the MDM team and related technology teams. Communicate complex technical concepts clearly to technical, operational and senior stakeholder audiences, influencing decision-making and building confidence in platform direction, reliability and governance. Align with and actively demonstrate the UNSW Code of Conduct and Values. Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the University and take reasonable care to ensure that actions or omissions do not adversely impact the psychosocial or physical health and safety of yourself or others. Ensure hazards and risks, psychosocial and physical, are identified and controlled for tasks, projects and activities that pose a health and safety risk within your area of responsibility. Who you are: Tertiary qualification in Information Technology, Computer Science, Data Engineering, Cloud Engineering, Software Engineering or a related discipline, or equivalent professional experience in a complex enterprise technology environment. Extensive experience designing, supporting, administering or evolving enterprise data, integration or cloud platforms in a large and complex organisation. Demonstrated experience with Informatica Cloud Platform, Informatica IDMC/IICS and Master Data Management capabilities, preferably including Customer 360 or equivalent MDM solutions Strong understanding of Master Data Management concepts, including master data modelling, data quality, metadata, lineage, survivorship, reference data, governance and secure data access Proven ability to design and deliver secure, scalable and supportable platform solutions with strong ownership, accountability and engineering discipline Demonstrated experience working with Azure cloud services, including integration, compute, networking, identity, security, monitoring and data platform services. Working knowledge of relevant Azure data and integration services such as Azure Data Factory, Azure Databricks, Azure Synapse, Azure Data Lake, Azure Storage, Azure Service Bus and MS SQL. Working knowledge of AWS concepts and services relevant to hybrid cloud integration, including IAM, VPC/networking, security groups, S3 and CloudWatch. Strong understanding of enterprise integration patterns, APIs, REST/JSON services, connectors, event-driven integration and secure data exchange between systems Proven experience in platform operations and service reliability, including production support, incident management, problem management, service requests, monitoring, alerting, troubleshooting, root cause analysis and continuous operational improvement. Demonstrated understanding of DevSecOps principles and practices, including environment management, deployment controls, CI/CD alignment, monitoring, logging, scripting, automation and infrastructure-as-code practices. Strong understanding of cybersecurity, privacy, identity and access management, data protection, API security and operational resilience controls in enterprise platform environments. Ability to translate complex and ambiguous business, data and technical requirements into clear platform approaches, practical engineering patterns and value-driven outcomes Strong analytical and systems-thinking capability, with the ability to assess platform dependencies, data flows, integration points, operational risks and technical constraints across a complex technology ecosystem. Excellent stakeholder engagement and communication skills, with the ability to build trust, guide decision-making and explain complex technical concepts to technical, operational and senior business audiences. Strong business acumen, with the ability to balance business value, feasibility, risk, security, operational supportability and long-term enterprise sustainability when shaping platform solutions. Demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively with architects, cybersecurity, cloud, application, data, integration and business teams to deliver secure and sustainable platform outcomes. Experience developing reusable patterns, standards, runbooks, monitoring frameworks, operational playbooks or automation scripts that improve consistency, reliability and delivery efficiency. Demonstrated ability to mentor peers, share knowledge and uplift technical capability across cross-functional teams. Curiosity and willingness to continuously learn emerging Informatica, cloud, data management, integration, automation and AI-adjacent technologies, with the ability to assess their relevance and apply them responsibly in practical settings. Strong commitment to UNSW’s values, objectives, policies and standards. Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities, including psychosocial and physical safety obligations, and commitment to undertaking relevant health and safety training. Benefits and Culture Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits How to Apply: please apply through the portal, we would like you to submit a full application including resume and addressing the who you are section. Applications close: Monday 14th of September at 11.30pm Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Get in Touch: Jen MacLachlan j.maclachlan@unsw.edu.au Talent Acquisition Partner – UNSW IT Please apply through the application portal and not via the contact above. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 01 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 14 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542170/education-manager": {
      "title": "Education Manager",
      "description": "Education Manager Apply now Job no: 542170 Work type: Full Time Location: Canberra, ACT Categories: Project Management, Management Education Manager, UNSW Canberra Employment type: Fixed-term contract, full-time (35 hours) Duration: 12 months Remuneration: Level 8 from $132,445 + leave loading = 17% super Location : UNSW Canberra, City Campus Work rights required: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. About UNSW Canberra The UNSW Public Policy Institute (PPI) is an education-focused centre for advancing policymaking and public leadership, dedicated to strengthening the capability of governments to address complex policy challenges and deliver public value. PPI is located at the Canberra city precinct and draws on the breadth of world-class research and expertise from across UNSW to inform and strengthen policy education. The PPI brings together academic experts, practitioners and facilitators to support the design and delivery of effective, evidence-led policy. University of New South Wales (UNSW) in Canberra has multiple locations in the Nation’s Capital. UNSW Canberra distinguishes itself from other institutions by its commitment to being thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all endeavours. This combined approach is integral to the university's impact and contributes to its recognition as one of the top 20 universities globally, as well as a proud member of Australia's esteemed Group of Eight. Why Your Role Matters: This is an exciting period of growth for our new Institute of Public Policy. The Education Manager is responsible for leading the planning, development, implementation and continuous improvement of the Institute of Public Policy's education portfolio. The role ensures the Institute delivers high-quality, professionally relevant education programs that support UNSW's strategic objectives to establish the Institute as Australia's leading provider of applied public policy education. The position leads the operational management of the education portfolio, including program development, quality assurance, educational systems, academic operations and continuous improvement. programs while ensuring an outstanding learner experience. The Education Manager leads to the strategic growth of the Institute's education portfolio through effective planning, stakeholder engagement, governance and continuous innovation. Accountabilities: The Education Manager operates with significant autonomy to manage the Institute's education operations and contributes to the development of the education portfolio. This is a professional, as opposed to academic role. Our successful candidate will not be expected to contribute to the content of the courses but the structure, process and frameworks of the Institute. Who You Are (skills and experience): • Relevant tertiary qualification and or post graduate qualification with substantial experience in higher education, executive education or professional learning, or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience. Post graduate qualifications are highly regarded. • Demonstrated experience managing education programs from planning through delivery and evaluation. • Experience developing operational systems and processes supporting education delivery. • Demonstrated knowledge of curriculum implementation, education quality assurance and continuous improvement. • Experience coordinating multiple education projects and managing competing priorities. • Strong stakeholder engagement skills with the ability to work collaboratively across academic and professional teams. • Demonstrated ability to analyse education performance data and implement improvements. • Well-developed project management, organisational and communication skills. • Experience supervising staff or coordinating multidisciplinary teams. Benefits and Culture: UNSW is committed to helping staff balance work-life responsibilities, by providing access to high-quality services, facilities, and flexible work and leave arrangements. Flexible working options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Additional three days leave during December festive period Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice Free gym access UNSW-wide strategy to focus on Healthy Body, Healthy Mind, Healthy Places and Healthy Culture. You can learn more about our Benefits and Culture at Lifestyle & Benefits UNSW Eligibility: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. How to Apply: Make each day matter with a meaningful career at UNSW. Submit your application online before Tuesday 15 September 2026 at 11:30pm , including your CV and a 1–2-page cover letter addressing the required skills and experience outlined in the position description. In order to view the position description, please ensure that you allow pop-ups for https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au Get in Touch: For role-related queries please contact: Jessica Genauer - Academic Director j.genauer@unsw.edu.au For recruitment process-related queries please contact: Holly Whale – Talent Acquisition Partner h.whale@unsw.edu.au Applications cannot be accepted if sent directly to the contact listed. Find out more about the lifestyle and benefits when working with UNSW For further information about UNSW Canberra, please visit: UNSW Canberra For further information on living in Canberra, please visit: Living in Canberra UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 01 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 15 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542276/lead-business-analyst-program": {
      "title": "Lead Business Analyst - Program",
      "description": "Lead Business Analyst - Program Apply now Job no: 542276 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Project Management, Analyst Employment Type: Full Time, Fixed Term, 2-year opportunity Remuneration: Competitive Total Remuneration Package Location: Kensington with transition to newly renovated office at our Randwick campus, anticipated for October 2026 About UNSW At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work.  We aspire to be Australia’s global university, improving and transforming lives through excellence in research, outstanding education and a commitment to advancing a just society. About the Role The Lead Business Analyst leads business analysis and functional design for the Business Optimisation Strategy (BOS) program across Finance, HR, and Procurement. The role evaluates current-state processes and systems to define streamlined, fit-for-purpose future-state solutions and designs. By partnering with architects, stream leads, IT stakeholders, ERP vendors, and external consultants, the role ensures end-to-end solution integrity and delivery readiness. It plans BA activity, maintains high artifact quality, manages interdependencies, and provides decision support to strengthen program governance. Reporting to the Senior PMO Lead, the role carries no direct reports. Responsibilities Establish and execute the BA framework, including standards, modeling conventions, and quality gates across the BOS program. Lead discovery to analyse current-state processes, controls, data, and systems across Finance, HR, and Procurement. Develop future-state process designs and functional solution options aligned with BOS program objectives. Define and manage the BA work plan, aligning milestones, dependencies, and deliverables with the broader program roadmap. Maintain end-to-end requirement traceability from business objectives through to technical design, configuration, testing, and operational readiness. Partner with the ERP vendor and external consultants to shape, review, and validate functional designs for completeness and feasibility. Plan and facilitate workshops with key business and IT stakeholders to drive requirements, resolve gaps, and secure alignment. Identify and manage design risks and operational impacts while supporting program governance through transparent decision-making. About You Tertiary qualification in IT, Business, Commerce, or equivalent senior experience in large-scale transformation environments. 8+ years leading business analysis and functional design across complex enterprise platforms, spanning Finance, HR, and Procurement domains. Expertise in process improvement and standardisation, using contemporary modelling techniques (BPMN, user stories, data models, functional specifications). Strong planning and delivery management skills across Waterfall and Agile/Hybrid lifecycles, including dependency management and governance alignment. Proven track record of managing vendor and consulting partners, ensuring quality deliverable reviews, and resolving design gaps. Exceptional stakeholder facilitation, communication, and influence skills, combined with a commitment to organisational values, policies, and guidelines. For further information on the role & responsibilities, please refer to the Position Description. How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter detailing your interest and suitability for the job (as per the skills & experience bullet points in the job description ) before 11:55pm, Sunday 13 September 2026. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Additional 3 days leave during December festive period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Job queries contact: Tai James, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: tai.james@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Position Description Advertised: 01 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 13 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542535/service-designer-hybrid": {
      "title": "Service Designer (Hybrid)",
      "description": "Service Designer (Hybrid) Apply now Job no: 542535 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology, Strategy, Cyber Employment Type: Full-time (35 hours per week) Duration: Fixed-term contract till 30 June 2029 Remuneration: $153,933 - $163,338 + 17% super + leave loading Location: UNSW Kensington Campus – Hybrid work arrangement About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: UNSW is undertaking a major transformation of its Learning Management System (LMS) and the services, processes and user experiences that support teaching and learning across the University. As a Service Designer within the LMS Program, you will help shape how students, educators and professional staff interact with the LMS and related services. Reporting to the HCD/UX Manager, you will work across the Program to understand user needs, map current and future-state service experiences, and identify opportunities to improve the services, processes and interactions that support teaching and learning at UNSW. Working closely with Service Owners, business stakeholders, product teams, designers, engineers and vendors, you will translate insights into journey maps, service blueprints, service requirements and design artefacts that support program decision-making and delivery. You will help connect user needs with business objectives and technical implementation, translating research and insights into practical service requirements, service models and design recommendations that support effective decision-making and delivery outcomes. You will contribute to discovery activities, proof-of-concepts, prototypes and pilot initiatives to test usability, functionality, scalability and service viability. Working with engineering, UX/UI, architecture, cybersecurity and delivery teams, you will help ensure solutions meet user needs while considering operational requirements, service support models and long-term sustainability. This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to one of UNSW's largest digital transformation programs and help improve the LMS-enabled experiences of students, educators and staff across the University. Responsibilities Summary: Conduct user research and discovery activities to understand the needs, behaviours and pain points of students, educators, professional staff and support teams interacting with the LMS ecosystem. Support workshops, interviews, co-design sessions and stakeholder engagement activities to gather insights and inform service design outcomes. Develop service design artefacts including journey maps, service blueprints, personas, stakeholder maps and future-state service models. Co-design end-to-end technology-enabled service experiences across the LMS ecosystem and associated processes, consider user, business, technical and operational requirements. Translate research findings and user insights into practical recommendations, service requirements and design outputs that inform program and solution decisions. Work closely with Service Owners, business stakeholders, product teams and vendors to identify opportunities for service improvements and enhanced user experiences. Collaborate with engineering, UX/UI, architecture, cybersecurity and delivery teams to ensure service design outcomes are understood and reflected in solution design and implementation. Contribute to the development and evaluation of proof-of-concepts, prototypes and pilot activities to test usability, functionality, scalability and service viability. Assess and communicate service viability considerations, including user value, operational impacts, scalability and implementation readiness, to support information program decisions. Support the design of operational processes, service models and supporting practices required for successful implementation of LMS-related services. Consider service support, operational processes and transition requirements when designing future-state services and experiences to help ensure successful adoption and ongoing delivery. Analyse user feedback, service metrics, operational insights and research insights to identify opportunities for continuous improvement, measure service effectiveness and inform decision-making. Apply Human-Centred Design and Service Design methods and practices across the LMS Program to ensure solutions remain aligned to user and stakeholder needs. Create and maintain clear documentation and design artefacts that communicate insights, recommendations and service design outcomes to diverse stakeholder groups. Align with and actively demonstrate the UNSW Code of Conduct and Values. Identify and control psychosocial and physical health and safety risks associated with tasks, projects and activities within the area of responsibility. For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: 5+ years of experience in Service Design, Business Analysis, or a similar role. Experience applying service design practices within complex digital transformation, enterprise technology or large-scale change programs, ideally involving technology-enabled services or platforms. Strong understanding of Human-Centred Design methods, including user research, journey mapping, service blueprinting, workshop facilitation and co-design. Experience translating user needs, research findings and business requirements into practical service and experience improvements. Experience working collaboratively with business stakeholders, technical teams, vendors and delivery teams in complex environments. Strong communication, facilitation and stakeholder engagement skills, including the ability to build relationships across diverse stakeholder groups. Experience contributing to discovery activities, pilots, proof-of-concepts or iterative testing approaches that inform service and product decisions. Experience creating service design artefacts such as journey maps, service blueprints, personas, ecosystem maps and future-state service models. Understanding of operational service considerations, service support requirements and the factors that contribute to sustainable service delivery and continuous improvement. Understanding of technology delivery approaches and experience working in Agile, Scrum or iterative delivery environments. Familiarity with enterprise platforms, digital products or service ecosystems and the operational considerations required to support them. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills, with the ability to use qualitative and quantitative insights to inform decision-making. Ability to manage competing priorities and work effectively both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW's aims, objectives, values, relevant policies and health and safety responsibilities. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world.  Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) Mandatory Application Questions To be completed while completing and submitting the application form. Share a detailed example of how you uncovered the true user needs when people say one thing and do another? What Human-Centred Design prototypes, methods, and artefacts have you used most frequently (e.g. user research, journey mapping, service blueprinting, co-design workshops)? Describe how you test your designs with people who have differing abilities and backgrounds? How have you translated these user insights into requirements, design decisions or implemented solutions? What experience do you have working within large-scale digital transformation, enterprise technology or platform implementation programs? How to Apply: Submit your CV along with a cover letter addressing your suitability for the role via the application portal before 11:30pm on the 14 th September 2026. Get in Touch: For queries about the role reach out the LMS Program Manager, Timothy Hands on: timothy.hands@unsw.edu.au For queries regarding the recruitment process, reach out to the Talent Acquisition Partner, Ayla Kamal on: ayla.kamal@unsw.edu.au * Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 01 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 14 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542537/student-business-analyst-hybrid": {
      "title": "Student Business Analyst (Hybrid)",
      "description": "Student Business Analyst (Hybrid) Apply now Job no: 542537 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology, Student Services, Analyst Employment Type: Full-time (35 hours per week) Duration: Fixed-term contract (3 years) till Dec 2029 Remuneration: $132,445 - $149,228 + 17% super + leave loading Location: UNSW Kensington Campus – Hybrid Work Arrangements About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: UNSW is rolling out a new learning management system (LMS), approved in June 2026, as a major university-wide transformation by 2030. Delivered faculty by faculty and aligned with the 2028 Flex-Semester transition for early adopting faculties, it will reshape teaching and learning. The LMS Program goes beyond technology, combining change, training, and engagement through a human-centred design approach. Joining now means contributing to a high-impact initiative with strong leadership and a multidisciplinary team. As a Student Business Analyst, you will play a key role in shaping and delivering impactful digital experiences and coordinating student engagement across the University. Partnering closely with Human Centred Designers, students and stakeholders, you will analyse and define business requirements to support the development and enhancement of LMS transformation - driving meaningful improvements to student life. You will work in a fast-paced, collaborative scrum environment to translate business needs into practical, scalable solutions. Your expertise will help ensure that student voices and student learning lifecycle initiatives are aligned to strategic priorities, while continuously improving user experience, operational efficiency, and online engagement across the University. In this role, you will also lead and oversee business analysis engagements, ensuring high-quality outcomes across the transformation of the LMS ecosystem and initiatives. Acting as a trusted advisor, you will bring clarity to complex challenges, guide decision-making, and contribute to the continuous evolution of digital capabilities within the organisation. Responsibilities Summary: Lead student business analysis activities on the LMS Program including requirements gathering, process mapping, and solution design. Collaborate with stakeholders (particularly students) across faculties, divisions, and technical teams to understand business drivers, document requirements, and align outcomes with institutional priorities. Coordinate, document, and facilitate student LMS feedback, requirements, pain-points, and assessments as the “voice of the student”. Analyse and model “as-is” and “to-be” business processes and assess the operational and strategic impacts of proposed changes. Facilitate workshops, interviews, and other discovery activities to elicit and validate stakeholder needs and pain points. Define functional and non-functional requirements and ensure traceability throughout the delivery lifecycle. Contribute to the development of business case outcomes, including benefit analysis, risk assessments, and recommendations. Work closely with the LMS delivery teams to ensure feasibility and alignment with enterprise systems and integration standards. Support change management and human-centred design by identifying stakeholder (particular students) impacts and contributing to transition planning and training strategies. Participate in testing phases by defining test scenarios, supporting user acceptance testing (UAT), and validating deliverables. Contribute to continuous improvement of business analysis standards, templates, tools, and knowledge sharing across the organisation. Stay informed of higher education trends, technology developments, and best practices in digital transformation. For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. A minimum of 3 years professional experience in business analysis or equivalent business experience, including well-developed business and needs analysis skills. Knowledge   and   understanding   of   project   management   methods, tools   and   the   software development lifecycle (SDLC). Knowledge and competency in the use of professional business analysis techniques and methods (e.g. Use Case Analysis, UML). Well-developed skills in the use of standard office toolsets including word processors, spreadsheets (e.g. MS Excel), drawing/diagramming tools (e.g. Visio), presentation tools (e.g. PowerPoint) and project management tools (e.g. MS Project). Previous experience working in an LMS application environment (Moodle, Canvas, Brightspace, or Blackboard). Demonstrated commitment to success in providing effective solutions to business needs. Excellent oral and written communication and interpersonal skills. Demonstrated ability to build and maintain strong customer relationships with a diverse range of stakeholders. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world.  Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) Mandatory Application Questions To be completed while completing and submitting the application form. Describe your experience gathering and documenting business requirements for a technology or business change initiative. Provide an example of how you gathered feedback from end users, students, customers or a community group and used those insights to influence a solution, service or improvement. Outline your experience working with Learning Management Systems (e.g. Moodle, Canvas, Brightspace, Blackboard) or similar digital platforms. Why are you interested in the Student Business Analyst role and what would you bring to UNSW's LMS Transformation Program? How to Apply: Submit your CV along with a cover letter addressing your suitability for the role via the application portal before 11:30pm on the 14 th of September 2026. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. For queries regarding the role, contact the LMS Program Manager, Timothy Hands on: timothy.hands@unsw.edu.au For queries regarding the recruitment process, contact the Talent Acquisition Partner, Ayla Kamal on: ayla.kamal@unsw.edu.au *Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 01 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 14 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542587/delivery-resource-lead": {
      "title": "Delivery Resource Lead",
      "description": "Delivery Resource Lead Apply now Job no: 542587 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Project Management Employment Type: Full Time, Fixed Term 12-month opportunity. Remuneration: Level 9: from $153,933 + 17% superannuation and leave loading Location: Kensington with transition to newly renovated office at our Randwick campus, anticipated for October 2026 About UNSW At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work.  We aspire to be Australia’s global university, improving and transforming lives through excellence in research, outstanding education and a commitment to advancing a just society. About the Role The Delivery Resource Lead is a senior EPMO role embedded within a large, multi-year transformation program. It is accountable for program-level resource allocation and delivery governance, alongside portfolio-level resource demand visibility and governance frameworks. The role establishes an integrated view of resource demand, capacity, and forecasting to align with strategic priorities. At the program level, it manages allocation, resolves skill and capacity risks, and safeguards delivery. At the portfolio level, it develops scalable frameworks, processes, and reporting to drive consistent, risk-informed governance across multiple programs. By providing data-driven insights, scenario modelling, and executive reporting, the Delivery Resource Lead influences prioritisation, sequencing, and investment trade-offs. The program framework created in this role serves as a reference model for broader adoption, elevating resource governance maturity across the portfolio. Reporting to the EPMO Project Management Lead, the role has no direct reports but exerts strong influence across Program and Workstream Leadership, HR, and executive stakeholders. Responsibilities Lead the end-to-end delivery resource lifecycle, including role definition and approval, resource system administration, onboarding and mobilisation arrangements, and offboarding. Drive program resource allocation to safeguard delivery priorities and strategic objectives. Assess capacity, capability, and skill requirements to identify risks early and implement effective mitigations. Provide scenario modelling, executive reporting, and visual insights to guide program and portfolio decision-making. Resolve cross-workstream resource conflicts and advise leadership on constraint impacts regarding scope, timing, and feasibility. Partner with HR, Finance, and capability leads to close workforce gaps via recruitment, contractors, and secondments. Design, embed, and continuously refine scalable governance standards, tools, and reporting frameworks across the portfolio while maintaining compliance with university policies. About You Tertiary qualification or equivalent senior experience in program delivery, portfolio governance, or resource management within complex transformation environments. Proven expertise in strategic resource allocation, capacity planning, and demand/supply scenario modelling for large-scale programs. Demonstrated ability to design, implement, and scale repeatable resource management processes, governance frameworks, and executive dashboards. Strong stakeholder engagement and conflict resolution skills, with a track record of collaborating across program leadership, HR, and PMO functions. Deep understanding of project delivery disciplines, risk management, and EPMO governance structures to influence senior decision-making and trade-offs. Commitment to promoting organisational values, safety responsibilities, and relevant policies within a complex institutional framework. For further information on the role & responsibilities, please refer to the Position Description. How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter detailing your interest and suitability for the job (as per the skills & experience bullet points in the job description ) before 11:55pm Sunday 13 September 2026. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Additional 3 days leave during December festive period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Job queries contact: Tai James, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: tai.james@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Position Description Advertised: 01 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 13 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542702/external-communications-officer-equity-diversity-inclusion-edi": {
      "title": "External Communications Officer- Equity, Diversity & Inclusion (EDI)",
      "description": "External Communications Officer- Equity, Diversity & Inclusion (EDI) Apply now Job no: 542702 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Marketing / Communications Employment Type: Full time, continuing Remuneration: $118k-$128k (depending on experience) + 17% super + leave loading Location: Kensington, NSW (Hybrid Working) At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. We aspire to be Australia’s global university, improving and transforming lives through excellence in research, outstanding education, and a commitment to advancing a just society. The Division of Societal Impact, Equity and Engagement (SIEE) is responsible for supporting a diverse and inclusive staff and student community and working with partner communities and broader society towards positive change. The Division of Societal Impact, Equity and Engagement is also responsible for several KPIs for staff and student equity Why Your Role Matters We’re seeking Communications Officers (EDI) to drive engagement around UNSW's equity, diversity and inclusion agenda. In this dynamic role, you'll create and share compelling communications that engage staff, students, media and key stakeholders, helping to amplify the impact of our EDI initiatives across the University and beyond. From developing engaging content to managing publications and communications campaigns, you'll play a key role in shaping how our EDI story is told. Reporting to the Head of Communications, you’ll use your expertise in public relations, communications or journalism to craft messages that inspire and inform. Your day-to-day focus will be: Developing and delivering compelling, audience-focused communications that promote UNSW initiatives across internal and external channels. Providing expert communications advice to stakeholders, ensuring messages are effectively targeted, aligned with UNSW's brand, and delivered through the most appropriate channels. Supporting media relations, content creation and campaign delivery, including media enquiries, media releases, PR activities, launches, awards and key University initiatives. Coordinating communications performance, leverage insights to drive continuous improvement, and support the development and distribution of key University publications and reports Supporting the broader activities of the Corporate Communications team Who you are: As an experienced communications professional with a strong track record delivering engaging internal and external communications. You are confident in translating complex information into clear, impactful messaging, using data-driven insights to enhance communication outcomes and champion equity, diversity and inclusion initiatives. A relevant tertiary qualification and a depth of subsequent relevant experience or an equivalent level of knowledge gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Demonstrated experience assisting with developing, delivering and measuring external communications and branding programs within large and complex organisations. Demonstrated experience in distilling complex information into straight forward messages for communication to a diverse group of stakeholders, in support of strategic goals. Proven ability to liaise effectively with all levels of internal and external stakeholders, and exercise creativity and enthusiasm in the development of new initiatives. Excellent time management and organisational skills, with the ability to navigate through competing deadlines. Superior written and verbal communication skills and the proven ability to influence and negotiate with stakeholders. Advanced level of computer literacy with excellent skills in Microsoft Office applications and databases (e.g. CRM). To Apply : Join UNSW and help shape a brighter, more inclusive future – one project, one story, one impact at a time, please submit your CV, Cover Letter and an example piece of written content that you have created (i.e. article, blog post). Submit your interest via the online applicant portal before Wednesday 16 th September 2026, 11:55pm. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Additional 3 days leave during December shutdown period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Job queries contact: For queries on the recruitment process, please contact Katherine Molloy: katherine.molloy@unsw.edu.au Please note applications sent directly to the contacts above cannot be accepted. Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 01 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 16 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542829/enquiries-assistantcurrent-unsw-student-opportunity": {
      "title": "Enquiries Assistant(Current UNSW Student Opportunity)",
      "description": "Enquiries Assistant(Current UNSW Student Opportunity) Apply now Job no: 542829 Work type: Casual Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration, Student Services At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. Ranked among the top 50 universities globally, we attract the highest potential students from Australia and around the world. Our researchers tackle the biggest economic, business, and social challenges with partners around the world. Position Summary Future Student Recruitment is responsible for strengthening marketing and recruitment efforts, ensuring UNSW is the premier destination in the state for local students and the premier destination in the country for internationals. The Future Student Enquiries Assistant (Level 1) is responsible for providing accurate and timely advice to prospective students and other stakeholders via all relevant inbound and outbound contact channels and events. This involves understanding degree and admissions policies to effectively respond to routine enquiries, whether received by phone, web form, email or web chat, all while delivering a high level of customer service, meeting KPIs and ensuring that the team service level agreements (SLAs) are being achieved. The Future Student Enquiries Assistant reports to the FSR Enquiry Team Lead and has no direct reports. This role will: • Follow instructions from the team leader and undertake tasks according to procedures. • Provide accurate and timely information to prospective students and stakeholders via all inbound/outbound channels and events. • Achieve or exceed individual and team Key Performance Indicators (KPI's), measuring case resolution volume, customer satisfaction and adherence to Service Level Agreements (SLA’s). • Maintain up-to-date records of enquiries and communication with all applicants within the UNSW CRM. • Maintain up-to-date knowledge of University policies and procedures, particularly those relating to prospective student enquiries, including participating in regular training sessions. • Maintain understanding of UNSW and faculty brand core value propositions, leveraging these in conversations with prospective students. • Participate in high volume, targeted conversion calling campaigns with the support of a guided script, to drive applications, acceptances and enrolments. • Align with and actively demonstrate the UNSW Values in Action: Our Behaviours and the UNSW Code of Conduct. • Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the health and safety of yourself or others. A person engaged is generally expected to have the following skills and experience: • You MUST be a current UNSW student to apply. • Advanced written and verbal communication skills and the ability to liaise effectively with a variety of stakeholders. • Strong level of computer literacy, including familiarity with Microsoft Office applications and the ability to quickly grasp new programs. • Ability to work in a high volume, process-driven environment with variable workflow patterns, whilst maintaining accuracy and professionalism. • Demonstrated ability to work flexibly and productively as an effective team member and to follow direction. • An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. • Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks: National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. If you require any further information regarding the role, please contact Jeffrey Wang via fei.j.wang@unsw.edu.au . To submit an application for the role, please do so via the 'Apply Now' button. Please note, no applications will be accepted via email. Position Description Advertised: 01 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 15 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542365/research-associate": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Research Associate Apply now Job no: 542365 Work type: Full Time Location: Canberra, ACT Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate Research Associate, UNSW Canberra Employment type: Fixed-term contract, full-time Duration: 2 years Remuneration: Level A6 $118,467 + leave loading + 17% super Location : UNSW Canberra, ADFA Campus Work rights required: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. About UNSW Canberra University of New South Wales (UNSW) in Canberra has multiple locations in the Nation’s Capital. UNSW Canberra distinguishes itself from other institutions by its commitment to being thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all endeavours. This combined approach is integral to the university's impact and contributes to its recognition as one of the top 20 universities globally, as well as a proud member of Australia's esteemed Group of Eight. Choosing a career at UNSW means embracing an environment where thriving, facing challenges, and engaging in meaningful work are not just encouraged but integral to the university experience. If you seek a career where you can excel and contribute meaningfully, you've found the right place. At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. Why Your Role Matters: This position is funded by a Australian Research Council Discovery Grant. Situated within the School of Systems and Computing, the Research Associate will be part of research activity in multidisciplinary settings requiring skills in cryptography, machine learning and biometrics. The Research Associate will contribute to the development of research results and their publication in international journals and participate at national and/or international conference/s. Who You Are (skills and experience): A PhD in Computer Science or Math or a relevant area/discipline that complements the breadth of expertise of the School. Candidates with PhD thesis submissions are also welcome. It is expected the PhD degree will be obtained within a short time before the commencement of the job. A demonstrated ability to conduct innovative and independent research in the field. A record of publications in high-quality journals and/or conferences in the field. Some typical venues include, Crypto, Eurocrypt, CCS, NDSS, IEEE TIFS and IEEE TPAMI. A strong background in post-quantum cryptography or applied cryptography. A strong math background in related areas such as coding theory will also be considered. Capability of applying deep learning models and willingness to use them for biometrics research required in the project. Benefits and Culture: UNSW is committed to helping staff balance work-life responsibilities, by providing access to high-quality services, facilities, and flexible work and leave arrangements. Additional three days leave during December festive period Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice Free gym access UNSW-wide strategy to focus on Healthy Body, Healthy Mind, Healthy Places and Healthy Culture. You can learn more about our Benefits and Culture at Lifestyle & Benefits UNSW Eligibility: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. How to Apply: Make each day matter with a meaningful career at UNSW. Submit your application online before Monday 28 September 2026 at 11:30pm , including your CV and a 1–2-page cover letter addressing the required skills and experience outlined in the position description. In order to view the position description, please ensure that you allow pop-ups for https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au Get in Touch: For role-related queries please contact: Jiankun Hu - Professor jiankun.hu@unsw.edu.au For recruitment process-related queries please contact: Holly Whale – Talent Acquisition Partner h.whale@unsw.edu.au Applications cannot be accepted if sent directly to the contact listed. Find out more about the lifestyle and benefits when working with UNSW For further information about UNSW Canberra, please visit: UNSW Canberra For further information on living in Canberra, please visit: Living in Canberra UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 31 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 28 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542589/lecturer-nutrition-dietetics-education-focused": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Nutrition & Dietetics  (Education Focused)",
      "description": "Lecturer - Nutrition & Dietetics  (Education Focused) Apply now Job no: 542589 Work type: Part Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Lecturer UNSW School of Health Sciences Employment Type: Part time (17.5 hours per week) Duration: Continuing opportunity Remuneration: Level B from $133k plus clinical loading, 17% superannuation contribution and annual leave loading (pro rata) Location: UNSW Kensington Campus, Sydney Working rights: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. Details of the role UNSW’s Education Focused roles (EF) enable academics to specialise in education and devote their time to delivering high-quality teaching and pursuing initiatives to enhance the educational experience of our students. Those who are appointed to this prestigious specialism will be expected to successfully drive educational excellence within the university’s teaching and learning communities. The Lecturer - Nutrition & Dietetics will make a significant contribution to the development and delivery of education in the Nutrition, Dietetics and Food Innovation program with a focus on the clinical stream of the program. This is an exciting opportunity for an educator and clinician to shape the learning experience of future nutrition and dietetics professionals. Working across the clinical stream, work-integrated learning content and placement program, you will contribute to innovative teaching, curriculum development and student success. The role suits someone who is passionate about educational excellence, committed to evidence-based practice and eager to support students in achieving strong academic, professional and employability outcomes. You will collaborate with colleagues across the discipline, bring current clinical and industry knowledge into the classroom, and contribute to a dynamic learning environment that prepares graduates for professional practice. The role of Lecturer reports to the Discipline Lead in their discipline, and has no direct reports. Some Key Skills Required Accredited Practicing Dietitian (APD) or eligibility and willingness to seek accreditation as an APD A PhD in Nutrition and Dietetics or other related health / medical disciplines is preferred although significant and relevant experience will be considered in lieu of PhD Demonstrated experience with industry and work-integrated learning and clinical education Demonstrated clinical experience Actively stays up to date with discipline knowledge and professional development Demonstrated experience in teaching and learning design, development and delivery at undergraduate and/or postgraduate level Experience using and/or designing with educational technologies and online delivery methods Evidence of teaching effectiveness and passion for educational excellence (e.g. relevant discipline-based curriculum design and development at a variety of levels and scales) Demonstrated knowledge of industry or experience of work-integrated learning (desirable) Evidence of highly developed interpersonal and organisational skills Evidence of ability to support and inspire students from diverse backgrounds and support student equity, diversity and inclusion initiatives. To learn more about this position and view the full list of criteria, a copy of the Position Description can be accessed by clicking on the position description button below this ad. Benefits and Culture People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world.  Our benefits include: Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply Please click on Apply now to apply online. Please provide a resume and a separate document addressing the skills and experience listed in the Position Description. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW . You should systematically address the selection criteria listed underneath the Skills & Experience heading within the position description in your application. Applications close:  Friday 25 th September 2026 before 11.30pm Sydney time Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Get in Touch For further information about the position please contact Professor Marijka Batterham, Discipline Lead, Nutrition, Dietetics and Food Innovation m.batterham@unsw.edu.au For further information about the recruitment process please contact Anabel Wason, TA Partner on a.wason@unsw.edu.au Please apply through the application portal and not via the contacts above. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 31 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 25 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/541645/product-manager-it-services": {
      "title": "Product Manager - IT Services",
      "description": "Product Manager - IT Services Apply now Job no: 541645 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology Full time continuing role as a Product Manager – IT Services Starting salary: $153,933 plus generous (17%) Super and Leave loading Location: Kensington campus in NSW (Hybrid Flexible Working) 2-3 days in the office About UNSW: Join Australia’s #1 university, aqnd a Global top 20 university (2027 QS World University Rankings)⁰. It is a place where talented people come together to solve real-world challenges and create meaningful impact. Our scale, diversity and ambition set us apart. We are a community of thinkers, makers and problem-solvers—thoughtful, practical and purposeful in how we work. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why this role matters: As a Product Manager - IT Services, you will play a critical role in shaping the end-to-end customer experience for enterprise-wide IT services. You will collaborate with cross-functional teams to design, prototype, and deliver user-centric services that meet the needs of our customers and drive business success. The Product Manager - IT Services plays an essential role in adoption of platforms and products by creating seamless, client-centric experiences. You will design the service ecosystem and deliver the change needed to make services easy to use and attractive to our uses. The role reports to Senior Manager – Platforms and Services. Accountabilities for this role include: Design Enterprise-wide IT services for a wide-ranging set of capabilities at UNSW Identify Service Request requirements with key stakeholders and design Service Request forms; identify any approval requirements, design support workflows, write use cases and test cases to validate stakeholder needs. Collaborate with product managers, engineers, and other stakeholders to ensure alignment and feasibility. Develop and maintain design documentation, including design principles, patterns, and standards. and Conduct usability testing and gather feedback to iterate and improve service designs. Develop and iterate on service concepts, including customer interfaces, workflows, and interactions, to support a whole of UNSW Technology cohesive and client-centric service experience. Develop user personas, journey maps, and service blueprints to inform design decisions Develop and document service management plans, hand-offs in DevSecOps processes and facilitate communication between teams to create change and operations understanding between teams. Prototype and test service concepts with clients to gather feedback and validate design decisions before implementation; Support implementation, go-live and postproduction change. Facilitate communication and collaboration between different team members and stakeholders to ensure a consistent and holistic approach to services for enterprise products and services Proactively identify and lead opportunities for operational improvements and implement changes. Share knowledge and provide training to promote cross-functional collaboration and skill development within the team. Who you are: Team player and can collaborate across end users, business partners, technology teams. Deep expertise and experience in working fast within a large enterprise environment. Significant individual contributor and ability to deliver quick incremental changes, documentation and communication with customers and developers. Significant understanding of developer experience in AWS and Azure Experience in service design and process improvement, with a focus on Human-Centred Design principles including Double Diamond design process model. Proven experience in service management design, specifically in creating and maintaining services across infrastructure and application domains. Excellent communication, collaboration, and project management skills. Familiarity with ITIL or other IT service management frameworks. Experience working with cross-functional teams, including Product Managers, engineers, and business stakeholders, to ensure alignment of service design with overall product vision and objectives. Knowledge of customer research methods and tools, such as interviews, surveys, and usability testing, to inform design decisions. Familiarity with security practices and frameworks relevant to cloud and hybrid environments. Understanding DevSecOps principles and practices. Continuous learning mindset to stay up to date with industry trends, emerging technologies, and best practices in service design and cloud/hybrid infrastructure services. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Pre-employment checks required for this position Tertiary qualification in Computer Technology (or equivalent), supplemented with demonstrated success in an IT environment and managing multiple complex projects in a large and diverse organisation and subsequent relevant experience. Benefits and Culture • Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) • Career development opportunities • 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments • Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period • Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits To apply:  Please submit your CV, Cover Letter and responses addressing the required Who you are criteria. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role; valid Australian working rights are required on application. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact: Jen MacLachlan - Talent Acquisition Partner- e: j.maclachlan@unsw.edu.au Please apply through the application portal as we do not accept direct applications to the email above. Applications close: Thursday 10th of September at 11.30pm UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 28 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 10 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542410/cyber-security-assurance-testing-lead": {
      "title": "Cyber Security Assurance Testing Lead",
      "description": "Cyber Security Assurance Testing Lead Apply now Job no: 542410 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Cyber • Full time continuing role within UNSW IT, Cyber Directorate – Cyber Security Assurance Testing Lead • Starting Salary $153,933 plus generous superannuation and leave loading • Kensington, Sydney location, 2-3 days in the office, Hybrid working About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. It’s the reason we’re one of the top 20 universities in the world (QS top 20) and a member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. The Cyber Security Assurance Testing Lead maintains, delivers and administers Cyber Security Assurance Services within a fit-for-purpose and adaptive Cyber Security Risk Management framework. The role is responsible for the assurance testing of ICT services and IT initiatives, and the provision of cyber security subject matter expertise, controls assurance, and reporting services to university stakeholders. The Cyber Security Assurance Testing Lead reports to the Cyber Security Controls Assurance Manager and has no direct reports. Accountabilities: • Ensure that high and medium cyber risk rated information resources and services are regularly subjected to security testing which includes penetration tests, web application scanning, dynamic application security testing and API testing. • Proactively conduct automated penetration testing and vulnerability assessments of new and existing applications, systems, and networks using relevant tools. • Facilitate annual controls assurance testing including but not limited to annual penetration testing of high cyber risk rated assets, red teaming exercises, etc, with the support of cyber security analysts, internal team members and external testing partners. • Review, validate and triage findings from the manual and automated penetration tests, vulnerability scans, app security testing, CSPM tools, API testing, etc. • Update the cyber risk register with risks from controls assurance tests. • Communicate and report on controls assurance testing findings and associated risks, and work with business owners, control owners and operators to remediate and close findings/issues as per vulnerability remediation timeframes stipulated in in the University’s security standards. • Ensure the accurate and timely release of controls assurance testing reporting and metrics. • Regularly review threat and vulnerability advisories from various sources (e.g. ACSC, NCSC, CISA) and, where a high priority response is required across the organisation, collaborate with the Cyber Security Operations teams. • Contribute to the ongoing development and execution of a continuous control’s assurance testing approach, including monitoring, control uplift (incl. automation) and rationalisation. • Support the independent audit of cyber security controls on behalf of the University, including statutory audits completed by the Audit Office of NSW. • Maintain awareness of changes to legal, regulatory compliance and contractual obligations that are relevant to the University’s management of cyber security risks. • Promote awareness of the University’s internal and external environment for emerging cyber security threats. • Build effective working relationship with internal and external stakeholders to develop innovative solutions that meet business needs. • Promote a culture of continuous improvement, championing professional standards, innovation, and methods. • Other duties appropriate and in line with to this position as requested by the Cyber Security Controls Assurance Manager or the Cyber Security leadership team. • Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values • Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the psychosocial or physical health and safety of yourself or others. • Ensure hazards and risks psychosocial and physical are identified and controlled for tasks, projects, and activities that pose a health and safety risk within your area of responsibility. Candidates do not need to meet every criterion to apply. We will consider equivalent skills and experience gained through diverse career paths, professional development, technical training and practical experience, alongside relevant desirable experience. • 6+ years of IT security services experience, 3+ years of experience in penetration testing, vulnerability management, application security testing, source code review. • Experience in 1st line assurance role, working with vulnerability management and scanning systems. • Scripting in PowerShell, Python, Bash, etc is advantageous. • AWS, Azure and Microsoft365 security experience desirable but not mandatory. • A relevant degree with extensive experience in cyber security operations or assurance teams within major organisations or an equivalent level of knowledge gained through any other combination of education, training, and experience. • Excellent understanding of industry-wide security standards and compliance frameworks such as ISO 27001, NIST 800-53, OWASP, CSA, Essential 8, PCI DSS, COBIT 5, Mitre ATT&CK etc. • Relevant industry certification(s) such as SANS certifications, CEH, OSCP, CompTIA Security+, and cloud platform certifications such as, AWS Security Speciality, Microsoft Azure (highly desirable). • Strong interpersonal, communication and negotiation skills including ability to develop effective relationships and influence key stakeholders at all levels in the organisation. • Analytical ability to present with credibility and translate technical and complex information concisely for diverse audiences using strong analytical and problem-solving skills. • Demonstrated high level of personal motivation, resilience, and ability to work effectively individually or in teams. • Experience in the use of automated vulnerability scanning, security validation, penetration testing, static and dynamic application security testing, and cloud posture management tools e.g. such as Pentera, Tenable.io, Checkmarx, Lacework, GuardDuty etc. • Ability to code in PowerShell, Python, Bash, etc is advantageous. • An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. • Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Pre-employment checks required for this position Benefits and Culture • Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) • Career development opportunities • 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments • Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period • Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits To apply:  Please submit your CV, Cover Letter and responses addressing the required criteria. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role; valid Australian working rights are required on application. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact: Jen MacLachlan - Talent Acquisition Partner- e: j.maclachlan@unsw.edu.au Please apply through the application portal as we do not accept direct applications to the email above. Applications close: Thursday 10th of September at 11.30pm UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 28 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 10 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542427/hdr-coordinator": {
      "title": "HDR Coordinator",
      "description": "HDR Coordinator Apply now Job no: 542427 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration, Student Services, Student Recruitment / Admissions HDR Coordinator (Faculty of Medicine and Health) Employment Type: Full – time (35 hours a week) Duration: Fixed term until 31 st August 2027 Remuneration : $108,021 base, plus 17% superannuation and leave loading Work rights required: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. Location: Kensington, Sydney About UNSW: UNSW Medicine & Health is a national leader in learning, teaching and research, with close affiliations to several of Australia’s finest hospitals, research institutes and health care organisations. With a strong presence at UNSW Kensington campus, the faculty have staff and students in teaching hospitals in Sydney as well as regional and rural areas of NSW including Albury/Wodonga, Wagga Wagga, Coffs Harbour and Port Macquarie. Details of the role The Higher Degree Research (HDR) Coordinator is responsible for all aspects of HDR candidate administration, and for providing the highest quality service and support to current and prospective HDR candidates, Postgraduate Coordinators and supervisors. The role has an emphasis on enhancing HDR candidate experience and is a lead contributor to a culture of continuous improvement in HDR coordination. A strong emphasis is also placed on the role to achieve effective working relationships and working as part of a team with other HDR Coordinators in the Faculty. This role plays a central part in supporting the research training lifecycle, ensuring HDR candidates and supervisors receive seamless, high-quality guidance from admission through to graduation. Working closely with academic leaders and professional colleagues, you will coordinate complex candidature processes, provide trusted advice on research administration matters, and contribute to initiatives that enhance the overall HDR experience. The position suits someone who enjoys managing multiple priorities, navigating sensitive or complex issues with professionalism, and building strong relationships across diverse stakeholder groups. You will also contribute to faculty-wide projects, communications, and events. The role reports to the Senior HDR Coordinator, supporting the Associate Dean (Research Training) and Postgraduate Coordinators of allocated Schools. The role has no direct reports. Some key skills required: Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Coordinate all aspects of postgraduate research administration for researchers from assigned Schools and/or Centres, delivering an efficient support service across enrolment, scholarships, progression and graduation processes. Provide advice, guidance and administrative support to School Managers, Heads of School and Postgraduate Coordinators on a range of complex candidature issues. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively and productively within a team, and to take initiative and work independently while managing competing demands. Highly advanced interpersonal, written and oral communication skills. Demonstrated experience providing effective customer service and support to clients at all levels. Coordinate the research progress review process, ensuring participation of appropriate panel members and overseeing completion of associated tasks. Coordinate development, reviews, updates and maintenance of communications and marketing materials related to the HDR program, including website and intranet content. Please refer to the position description for full details. UNSW Benefits and Culture 17% Superannuation and leave loading​ Flexible working ​ Additional 3 days of leave over the Christmas Period​ Access to lifelong learning and career development ​ Progressive HR practices​ More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here. Pre-Employment Checks: Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks: National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. How to apply: Please click on Apply now to apply online. Applications should not be sent to the contact listed below. Please provide a resume and a separate document addressing the skills and experience listed in the Position Description. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW . Contact Frank McHenry, Talent Acquisition Associate E: f.mchenry@unsw.edu.au Applications close: Sunday 13 th September before 11.30pm Sydney time UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 28 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 13 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542649/senior-business-analyst": {
      "title": "Senior Business Analyst",
      "description": "Senior Business Analyst Apply now Job no: 542649 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Analyst Employment Type: Full Time (35 hours per week), 12-Month Fixed Term Contract Remuneration: Level 9: From $153,933 + 17% superannuation + leave loading Location: UNSW Kensington Campus, NSW (hybrid) About UNSW UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. It’s the reason we’re one of the top 20 universities in the world and a member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters The Senior Business Analyst leads complex business analysis activities primarily in support of the UNSW India Program, with a focus on the student journey & experience. Working closely with both professional and academic staff, the role is responsible for student journey business needs, processes, and data requirements to ensure solutions are practical, scalable, and aligned with UNSW’s strategic objectives. The role influences business and technology decisions, supports the enablement of teaching and learning for transnational education ventures. The Senior Business Analyst reports to the BA Manager and has no direct reports. Key Responsibilities: Undertake business analysis activities for transnational education, supporting the definition and delivery of student journey related processes and systems. Partner with academic and professional staff to identify, analyse, and document transnational education venture business needs aligned to UNSW’s strategic objectives. Plan, prepare and facilitate workshops to define scope, elicit requirements, and build stakeholder alignment across University and Program teams. Support the development of scope briefs and contribute to business cases and options analysis. Translate business needs into clear requirements and user stories, ensuring traceability through to delivery. Develop solution briefs outlining proposed approaches, process impacts, and data requirements. Design and document work plans for your assigned deliverables. Build strong stakeholder relationships and collaborate with technology and delivery teams to ensure fit-for-purpose outcomes. Identify, document and actively manage dependencies across streams, escalating risks and issues where resolution is needed. Who You Are: You bring strong experience as a Senior Business Analyst within higher education institutions or other complex institutions. You have experience delivering business analysis across large, complex organisations and leading strategically significant initiatives. You are experienced in establishing work plans and driving to deliver them to agreed quality standards and timeframes with limited day to day direction. You are experienced in requirements elicitation, solution design, data analysis, and stakeholder engagement, and are comfortable working in structured delivery environments. You communicate and facilitate effectively with academic staff, professional teams, technical specialists, and senior stakeholders, bringing strong analytical, critical thinking and problem-solving skills. Relevant tertiary qualifications and familiarity with enterprise systems are desirable. For further details on role responsibilities and skills & experience required, refer to the position description found under the position listing on Jobs@UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible working options. Career development opportunities. 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments. Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness). For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits How to Apply: Submit your CV & a succinct 1-2 page cover letter detailing your interest and suitability for the job (as per the skills & experience bullet points in the job description) by 11:55pm Sunday 6 September 2026. Please note UNSW reserves the right to close advertisements before this date. Please ensure you apply as soon as you are able. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks: National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 28 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 06 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/541734/change-analyst": {
      "title": "Change Analyst",
      "description": "Change Analyst Apply now Job no: 541734 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Analyst Employment Type: Full Time, 35 hours per week, Fixed Term 2-year contract. Remuneration: Level 8 - from $132,445 plus 17% superannuation and leave loading Location: Kensington with transition to newly renovated office at our Randwick campus, anticipated for October 2026 About UNSW At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work.  We aspire to be Australia’s global university, improving and transforming lives through excellence in research, outstanding education and a commitment to advancing a just society. About the Role The Change Analyst drives end-to-end change management and business readiness across people, processes, technology, and policy. Working alongside project leads, BAU teams, and executives, this role translates solution requirements into clear impacts, prepares stakeholders, and ensures new ways of working are smoothly adopted. Additionally, the role sets practice standards, mentors change contributors and maintains high quality across all change initiatives. This position works within the P&C Stream of the EPMO, reporting to the Engagement and Readiness Lead and collaborates with Change Managers, Project Leads, Product Owners, and Business Sponsors. Responsibilities Lead end-to-end change impact and readiness assessments, identifying risks, dependencies, and actionable recommendations to inform transition decisions. Develop and execute integrated change and communication plans, proactively adjusting strategy as project scope, timelines, or priorities evolve. This includes preparing clear, visually compelling presentations using Microsoft PowerPoint. Design and deliver targeted training, capability uplift, and support models to equip teams and ensure long-term solution adoption. Facilitate stakeholder workshops, drive cross-functional alignment, and support User Acceptance Testing (UAT) to validate solution readiness. Track adoption metrics, feedback, and behavioural indicators to target interventions and embed new ways of working into standard operations. Champion safety, organizational values, and risk management by proactively controlling physical and psychosocial hazards within your scope. About You Demonstrated experience in organisational change management leading complex, multi-disciplinary initiatives across the full project lifecycle. Proven ability to independently conduct change and business readiness assessments, execute integrated change plans, and drive measurable adoption. Strong analytical, time management, and problem-solving skills, with advanced proficiency in standard office tools and web content management systems. Exceptional facilitation and communication skills, with the ability to build cross-functional relationships, engage diverse stakeholders, and translate technical concepts for end users. Demonstrated stakeholder management skills, including the ability to identify and analyse stakeholders, build trusted relationships, influence outcomes, manage differing perspectives, and maintain engagement through complex change. Strong presentation skills, with demonstrated ability to develop polished, audience-focused Microsoft PowerPoint presentations and confidently present complex information to executive, technical, and business audiences. Track record in designing and rolling out targeted training strategies, e-learning content, and ongoing support models. Commitment to organisational values, policies, and health and safety responsibilities, including managing both physical and psychosocial workplace risks. For further information on the role & responsibilities, please refer to the Position Description. How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter detailing your interest and suitability for the job (as per the skills & experience bullet points in the job description ) before 11:55pm, Wednesday 9 September 2026. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Additional 3 days leave during December festive period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Job queries contact: Tai James, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: tai.james@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Position Description Advertised: 27 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 09 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542385/research-strategy-support-manager-faculty-of-medicine-health": {
      "title": "Research Strategy & Support Manager- Faculty of Medicine & Health",
      "description": "Research Strategy & Support Manager- Faculty of Medicine & Health Apply now Job no: 542385 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Research Infrastructure, Management, Clinical/Medical Research Employment Type: Full Time; 35 hours per week Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Competitive Remuneration Package – Senior Appointment Location: Kensington Campus, New South Wales . Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold unrestricted work rights to be considered for this position. Position Summary The Research Strategy and Support Manager is a senior leadership appointment within the Faculty of Medicine & Health , providing strategic and operational leadership of the Faculty’s research support and infrastructure functions. Partnering closely with Faculty leadership, the role shapes and drives the strategic direction of the Faculty’s research enterprise, aligning governance, investment, resources, systems and services to strengthen research performance, funding, impact, capability and HDR outcomes. This role requires a strong, ambitious and strategic leader who can operate effectively in a complex environment, influence at senior levels, and mentor and develop high-performing teams. The successful candidate will lead strategic transformation, identify emerging opportunities and risks, build organisational capability and establish strong partnerships across the University and externally. As a highly visible senior appointment with significant scope and influence, the role also provides strong career development and progression opportunities. Some key skills required: Extensive experience in research development, funding strategy, Higher Degree Research (HDR) support, or research operations within a complex organisation. Demonstrated experience shaping organisational strategy and exercising delegated authority to translate strategic priorities into major initiatives, governance frameworks and measurable organisational outcomes. Demonstrated ability to use data, reporting and analytics to inform decision-making, monitor performance and drive continuous improvement. Demonstrated executive leadership of large, multidisciplinary professional teams responsible for delivering strategically significant organisational outcomes within a complex matrix environment. Demonstrated strong operational management capability, including planning, priority setting, financial management, resource allocation and project management, in a complex high-volume environment. Demonstrated ability to lead organisational change initiatives, including embedding new processes, systems or operating models. Please refer to the position description for full details. Benefits and Culture: UNSW offer a competitive salary and access to a plethora of UNSW-perks including: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments 10 days paid cultural leave per year Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here How to Apply: Make each day matter with a meaningful career at UNSW. Submit your application including your resume & cover letter addressing the selection criteria, online before Thursday 17th September at 11:55pm. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, WWCC and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Get in Touch: Aarti a.aarti@unsw.edu.au Talent Acquisition Associate Please apply through the application portal and not via the contact above. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 27 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 17 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542527/financial-control-accountant": {
      "title": "Financial Control Accountant",
      "description": "Financial Control Accountant Apply now Job no: 542527 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Finance Employment Type: Full-time (35 hours per week) Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Level 8: $132,445 - $149,228 + 17% super + Leave Loading Location: UNSW Kensington Campus About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: The Financial Control & Compliance function is responsible for UNSW’s university-wide financial control environment and associated compliance policies and processes. This includes statutory financial reporting and external audit, controlled entity accounting and reporting, technical accounting advisory, infrastructure and asset accounting, and tax management and advisory. As the Financial Control Accountant, you will provide high-quality, specialised and complex accounting support across the Financial Control & Compliance function. You will help maintain and improve UNSW’s financial control and compliance environment through accurate reporting, technical accounting advice, stakeholder support and continuous improvement. The position reports to the relevant Manager and has no direct reports Responsibilities Summary: Prepare and deliver month-end, year-end and other financial reporting, ensuring accuracy and reporting deadlines are met. Provide accounting support, insights and advice to internal and external stakeholders, including technical accounting assessments and implementation of changes in accounting standards. Maintain contemporary technical accounting knowledge, including Australian Accounting Standards and Interpretations and relevant legislative and regulatory requirements. Act as a subject matter expert, providing accurate and timely responses to complex accounting questions and queries. Preparing technical accounting memos and preparing or reviewing complex areas. Support Group Financial Control requirements, including reviewing and monitoring significant transactions, balances and cashflows. Provide timely and effective advice on financial performance and financial operational issues. Liaise with internal and external auditors, providing guidance and support to assist with audit requirements. Identify and contribute to continuous improvement initiatives that improve the effectiveness and efficiency of key financial outcomes. For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: Tertiary qualification in Accounting, CA/CPA qualification and demonstrated successful experience in a similar accounting or financial role. Extensive technical accounting knowledge and experience applying relevant Australian Accounting Standards and Interpretations. Experience preparing technical accounting memos and preparing or reviewing complex areas such as consolidation and investment accounting, cash flows or financial instruments. Demonstrated ability to analyse and resolve complex financial and ledger issues efficiently and within required timeframes. In-depth knowledge of Microsoft Excel and substantial experience using a variety of finance systems. Strong understanding of best-practice financial and organisational risk frameworks, including identifying financial risks and assessing and designing appropriate mitigating controls and policies. Strong attention to detail with the ability to prioritise multiple tasks and manage high volumes of complex issues. Strong communication and stakeholder management skills, including experience with engaging with senior management. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world.  Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Submit your CV along with a cover letter addressing your suitability for the role in relation to the selection criteria (listed under ‘Skills & Experience’ on the PD) via the application portal before 11:30pm on the 10 th of September. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Get in Touch: For queries regarding the recruitment process, contact Lucy Gerondis, Talent Acquisition Partner, UNSW: l.gerondis@unsw.edu.au *Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 27 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 10 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542619/venue-operations-assistant": {
      "title": "Venue Operations Assistant",
      "description": "Venue Operations Assistant Apply now Job no: 542619 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Hospitality & Events Employment Type: Full-time, continuing Remuneration: Starting from $84k + 17% super and leave loading Location: Kensington, NSW (On-campus role) About UNSW UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. Why Your Role Matters: As a key member of the Venue and Event Services team, you will help create exceptional experiences for event attendees by providing essential day-to-day support that ensure events and venues are delivered efficiently. Supporting the Venues and Events Manager, this role ensures every space is welcoming, professionally presented, and event-ready, while supporting the seamless delivery of a diverse range of meetings and events. Through coordinating venue setups, managing service requests, supporting casual event staff, and responding to client enquiries, you will play an important role in maintaining high service standards and operational continuity across our managed venues. Due to the nature of this role, occasional out of hours and weekend work is required aligned to event schedules across weekday evenings and weekends. This role will: Deliver exceptional front-of-house and onsite event operations to ensure  positive client and guest experiences. Ensure venues are professionally presented, event-ready, and maintained to high standards. Administer operational service requests and liaise with internal facilities stakeholders. Co-ordinate venue set ups and provide direction and assistance to casual crew. Contribute to venue and event services project activity and continuous improvement. Who you are: Holding service-focused experience with strong administrative capability, ideally in the venue or events space to support venue operations and event delivery in a fast-paced environment. Highly organised and detail-oriented, you excel at managing competing priorities, building positive stakeholder relationships, and ensuring exceptional service outcomes, alongside: High level of customer service. Great verbal and written communication skills. Strong understanding of administrative processes and digital applications Ability to solve problems following learnt procedures. Experience in working effectively individually, and as part of a team in a service-focused operational environment. Professional background within hospitality, venues, or events industry desirable. For further information on the role & responsibilities, please refer to the position description How to Apply: Submit your CV detailing your interest and suitability for the job before 13th September by 11:55pm. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this r o le , valid Australian ongoing working rights are required on application. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Additional 3 days leave during December shutdown period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Job queries contact: For queries on the recruitment process, please contact Katherine Molloy: katherine.molloy@unsw.edu.au Please note applications sent directly to the contacts above cannot be accepted. Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 27 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 13 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542668/postdoctoral-fellow-psychology": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow - Psychology",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Fellow - Psychology Apply now Job no: 542668 Work type: Part Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. The School of Psychology at UNSW is the leading Psychology school in Australia. It is located in the Faculty of Science and is recognised nationally and internationally for its excellence in research and teaching.  It offers world class undergraduate psychology degrees, as well as postgraduate professional Masters training in Clinical and Forensic Psychology, an online Graduate Diploma program, and a PhD research program with over 120 students. 40 academic staff conduct experimental research in many areas of Psychology, including behavioural and cognitive neuroscience, perception, learning, memory and cognition, as well as health, social, developmental, clinical and forensic psychology. UNSW Psychology is the only School in the southern hemisphere ranked in the top 30 by discipline in the THES, QS and ARWU rankings. The School has well equipped research and teaching facilities and attracts outstanding staff and students from Australia and overseas. UNSW is located 5 km from the centre of Sydney and is close to other research centres, teaching hospitals, transport, shopping, and beaches. For further information about the school please visit www.psychology.unsw.edu.au The Postdoctoral Fellow (Level A) is expected to contribute towards the research effort of UNSW and to develop their research expertise through the pursuit of defined projects relevant to their particular field of research. This role contributes to the research project “Improving mental health outcomes through parent and teacher engagement in school-based early intervention” in partnership with Sydney Catholic Schools. This project aims to evaluate the acceptability and effectiveness of school-adapted parent training intervention, matched to the individual needs of young children with disruptive behaviour problems, delivered within a Catholic school setting in South West Sydney. This position is based at the University of NSW in the School of Psychology, however the Postdoctoral Fellow will be required to travel regularly to South West Sydney. The Postdoctoral Fellow is responsible for overseeing day-to-day work of project staff, acting as a key coordinator for the project and undertaking typical research activities including data management & analysis and supporting the dissemination of research outcomes. About the role LEVEL A - $118k - $126k plus 17% Superannuation and annual leave loading Fixed Term – 2.5 years (to July 2029) Part time (17.5 hours) Specific responsibilities for this role include: Contribute independently or as a team member in collaborative research with a focus to enhance the quality of research outcomes in the discipline area. Conduct research (as per the norms of the discipline) and/or enable research teams to create scholarly output that is recognised by peers. This will include acting as a key coordinator for the trial, ensuring adherence to trial protocols and that project milestones and outcomes are met. Undertake specific research project/s under the guidance of a research leader and contribute to development of research activities. Support the dissemination of research outcomes through appropriate channels and outlets including the preparation and submission of reports, manuscripts, and presentations. Undertake discipline-appropriate research activities, e.g. surveys, literature reviews, data gathering and/or recording of results using appropriate research methods. Participate in and/or present at conferences and/or workshops relevant to the project as required. Assist with the supervision of research students in the research area where required. Develop and refine relevant study materials in accordance with International Council for Harmonisation Guidelines for Good Clinical Practice, the Consolidated Standards of Reporting Trials (CONSORT) framework, regulatory guidelines, and study objectives. Develop and maintain well-organised, efficient systems of scheduling, communication, file organisation and documentation of both meetings and study procedures and train project staff in their use. Embed these systems into day-to-day research operations to ensure high quality, compliant, and consistent data collection. Monitor adverse events and ensure they are properly documented and reported to the Sponsor(s) and Ethics Committee(s) as appropriate. Regularly travel to trial sites to perform training, on-site monitoring and coordination duties. Develop effective working relationships with and regularly liaise with multiple educational and other stakeholders and media outlets, and act as the first point of contact for internal and external stakeholders. Prepare and submit grant, ethics and governance applications. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values. Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the psychosocial or physical health and safety of yourself or others. About the successful applicant (Selection Criteria) A PhD in Psychology with AHPRA endorsement as a clinical, educational, or developmental psychologist. Demonstrated capacity for high-quality research, as evidenced by a strong track record relative to opportunity. Demonstrated experience in managing longitudinal research studies, clinical outcome, and/or randomised controlled research trials in clinical psychology. Demonstrated research coordination experience, including developing protocols, data management systems, governance, monitoring, and reporting. Excellent organisational skills, including an ability to undertake a range of diverse tasks, to work independently, accurately, and with attention to detail and to deliver high quality work on time. Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal skills and the ability to communicate effectively with a variety of stakeholders from diverse backgrounds and levels of seniority. Experience with research data management and statistical analysis using statistical software (SPSS). Demonstrated and applied knowledge/understanding of HREC and regulatory applications e.g. GCP and reporting for clinical trials. Demonstrated skills in project management, statistical software packages (eg. SPSS, R, MPlus) and statistical analysis. Bilingual capability in Arabic and English is highly desirable, particularly to support engagement with Arabic-speaking children and families. Ability and capacity to implement required UNSW health and safety policies and procedures. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. You should systematically address the selection criteria listed within the position description in your application. Please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed. Contact: Eva Kimonis E: e.kimonis@unsw.edu.au Applications close: September 24 th , 2026 Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 27 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 24 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542475/makerspace-and-electronics-manager": {
      "title": "Makerspace and Electronics Manager",
      "description": "Makerspace and Electronics Manager Apply now Job no: 542475 Work type: Full Time Location: Canberra, ACT Categories: Laboratory Technician, Engineer, Knowledge Exchange, Management Makerspace & Electronics Manager, UNSW Canberra Employment type: Full-time contract, 35 hours per week Duration: 12 months Remuneration: Level 8 from $ 132,445 + leave loading + 17% super Location : UNSW Canberra, ADFA Campus Work rights required: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. About UNSW Canberra University of New South Wales (UNSW) in Canberra has multiple locations in the Nation’s Capital. UNSW Canberra distinguishes itself from other institutions by its commitment to being thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all endeavours. This combined approach is integral to the university's impact and contributes to its recognition as one of the top 20 universities globally, as well as a proud member of Australia's esteemed Group of Eight. Choosing a career at UNSW means embracing an environment where thriving, facing challenges, and engaging in meaningful work are not just encouraged but integral to the university experience. If you seek a career where you can excel and contribute meaningfully, you've found the right place. At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. Why Your Role Matters: UNSW Canberra Makerspace provides a range of dedicated spaces designed to support hands-on learning, prototyping and engineering innovation. Our Makerspace and Electronics Manager leads the development, safe operation and service delivery of our Engineering Makerspaces and Electronics Laboratories. The role oversees facilities, equipment, training and programs that support electronics, digital fabrication, making, prototyping and project-based learning, creating an inclusive and collaborative environment in which students, staff and researchers can develop practical skills and translate ideas into working solutions. The position is also responsible for the delivery of professional electronics services to teaching and research, including the design, documentation, fabrication, testing, commissioning, maintenance and modification of electronic devices and systems. The role provides high-level technical and strategic advice, manages service priorities and oversees complex technical projects from requirements definition through to delivery and evaluation. Accountabilities: This is a varied role which you can really make your own. Student numbers are relatively low and thus engagement is high and rewarding. The main focuses are upon technical and leadership but also include: Makerspace strategy and service development Makerspace operations and user experience Teaching, training and innovation Engineering and technical service support Team leadership and stakeholder engagement Health, safety, compliance and governance Who You Are (skills and experience): • A relevant degree in electrical, electronic, mechatronic or a related engineering discipline with substantial relevant experience, including management and leadership experience; or an equivalent level of knowledge gained through a combination of education, training and experience. • Demonstrated experience managing a makerspace, prototyping facility, engineering workshop, laboratory or comparable complex technical service environment, including responsibility for staff, budgets, facilities, equipment and service delivery. • Demonstrated project management experience, including the development and implementation of plans, policies, procedures, systems, budgets, performance measures and technical documentation. • Demonstrated experience designing and delivering technical training, equipment inductions or competency-based learning for users with varied skills and experience. • Proven people leadership and management capability, including work planning, performance management, coaching, professional development and the effective allocation of priorities and resources. • Demonstrated ability to exercise sound judgement and initiative, solve complex technical and operational problems, manage competing priorities and work with a high level of autonomy within agreed objectives. • Excellent communication, consultation and interpersonal skills, with demonstrated ability to build productive relationships and provide high-quality, client-focused services to diverse stakeholders. • Demonstrated knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and experience implementing risk management, safe systems of work and compliance requirements in a technical environment. Benefits and Culture: UNSW is committed to helping staff balance work-life responsibilities, by providing access to high-quality services, facilities, and flexible work and leave arrangements. Flexible working options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Additional three days leave during December festive period Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice Free gym access UNSW-wide strategy to focus on Healthy Body, Healthy Mind, Healthy Places and Healthy Culture. You can learn more about our Benefits and Culture at Lifestyle & Benefits UNSW Eligibility: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. How to Apply: Make each day matter with a meaningful career at UNSW. Submit your application online before Wednesday 9 September 2026 at 11:30pm , including your CV and a 1–2-page cover letter addressing the required skills and experience outlined in the position description. In order to view the position description, please ensure that you allow pop-ups for https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au Get in Touch: For role-related queries please contact: Matt Barrett - Engineering Manager M-Barrett@unsw.edu.au For recruitment process-related queries please contact: Holly Whale – Talent Acquisition Partner h.whale@unsw.edu.au Applications cannot be accepted if sent directly to the contact listed. Find out more about the lifestyle and benefits when working with UNSW For further information about UNSW Canberra, please visit: UNSW Canberra For further information on living in Canberra, please visit: Living in Canberra UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 26 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 09 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542572/lecturer-or-senior-lecturer-functional-genomics": {
      "title": "Lecturer or Senior Lecturer - Functional Genomics",
      "description": "Lecturer or Senior Lecturer - Functional Genomics Apply now Job no: 542572 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Senior Lecturer, Lecturer One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. THIS ROLE IS LOCATED IN SYDNEY, AUSTRALIA. The Lecturer (Level B) / Senior Lecturer (Level C) is expected to carry out activities to develop their scholarly research and professional activities both nationally and internationally and to contribute significantly to achieving the teaching and service missions of the School of Biotechnology and Biomolecular Sciences and the Faculty of Science at UNSW. This role will lead a new research capability in functional genomics and disease modelling. Additionally, the role is expected to contribute to teaching in genetics and molecular biology courses, establish an independent, externally funded research program, and build a platform that enables the design, generation and interrogation of human disease models. About the role LEVEL B - $133 - $156 / LEVEL C - $161k - $185k plus 17% Superannuation and annual leave loading Fixed Term – 2 years – Convertible Tenure Track Full time (35 hours) Specific responsibilities for this role include: LEVEL B: Conduct research/scholarly activities under limited supervision, either independently or as a member of a team (as per the norms of the discipline) and design research projects. Contribute to the development of applications for competitive funding under the guidance of senior colleagues, including participating as co-investigator or chief investigator in competitive grant applications. Assist with delivery of a Predict-Perturb-Validate-Translate workflow using genomic, molecular and clinical data. Apply CRISPR or related perturbation methods, rescue/validation studies, pooled screens or arrayed screens. Develop quantitative readouts using methods such as transcriptomics, high-content imaging, flow cytometry, proteomics, single-cell analysis, drug response or other molecular/cellular phenotypes. Collaborate across UNSW, affiliated medical research institutes, hospitals, national infrastructure and industry to support platform use, translational opportunities and multidisciplinary projects. Contribute to undergraduate and postgraduate teaching in molecular biology, biotechnology, genomics, stem cell biology, genome engineering, disease modelling or related areas. Mentor and guide students and colleagues, develop the next generation of academics through involvement in supervision of HDRs and contribute to a collegial, inclusive and safe research culture. Apply a well-defined teaching philosophy and a critically reflective teaching practice that inspires student learning. Design and develop learning activities and resources, and provide assessment and feedback at both course and program level using a range of suitable approaches and learning environments. Make a positive contribution by leading School meetings and seminars and be a member of School/Faculty committees as required. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values. Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the health and safety of yourself or others. LEVEL C: (in addition to the above) Supervise HDRs to timely completion as primary supervisor; and contribute to HDR review panels. Establish and lead an independent research program in functional genomics and disease modelling, producing high-quality publications and competitive grant applications. Obtain research income through nationally competitive research grants (and/or research fellowships) and partnerships with research end-users, as a member or leader, at or above the level that is relevant to the discipline in leading universities. About the successful applicant (Selection Criteria) LEVEL B: A PhD in a related discipline, and/or relevant work experience. Demonstrated track record in functional genomics research with outcomes of high quality and high impact with clear evidence of the desire and ability to continually achieve research excellence as well as the capacity for research leadership. Experience with genome engineering or perturbation approaches, including CRISPR-Cas systems or related programmable perturbation technologies. Experience with isogenic iPSC disease models and/or organoid models is not essential but would be advantageous. Demonstrated experience in teaching and learning design, development and delivery at undergraduate and/or postgraduate level. Evidence of teaching effectiveness and passion for educational excellence (e.g. relevant discipline-based curriculum design and development at a variety of levels and scales). Evidence of ability to support and inspire students from diverse backgrounds and contribute to student equity, diversity and inclusion initiatives. Demonstrated ability to work in a team, collaborate across disciplines and build effective relationships. Evidence of highly developed interpersonal and organisational skills. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. LEVEL C: (in addition to the above) Experience in successfully recruiting and supervising high calibre students. Evidence of fostering teaching effectiveness and passion for educational excellence in others. Demonstrated success in initiating curriculum development and improvement. You should systematically address the selection criteria listed within the position description in your application. Please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed. Contact: Shane Grey E: s.grey@unsw.edu.au Applications close: September 23 rd , 2026 Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 26 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 23 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542599/senior-project-officer-research-support": {
      "title": "Senior Project Officer - Research Support",
      "description": "Senior Project Officer - Research Support Apply now Job no: 542599 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Project Management, Clinical/Medical Research Senior Project Officer Research Support- The Kirby Institute (Indigenous Targeted) Employment Type: Full - time (35 hours a week) Duration: 12 Months Fixed Term contract Remuneration : $118,483 base, plus 17% superannuation and leave loading Work rights required: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. Location: Kensington, Sydney Benefits: Cultural Leave and Indigenous Employee Network Whilst this role is open to everyone, we strongly encourage Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander to apply. About The Kirby Institute: The Kirby Institute is a world-leading health research institute at UNSW Sydney. We work to eliminate infectious diseases, globally. Our specialisation is in developing health solutions for the most at-risk communities. Putting communities at the heart of our research, we develop tests, treatments, cures and prevention strategies that have the greatest chance of success. Details of the role The Senior Project Officer plays a key role in supporting research projects as part of a multidisciplinary team of researchers in the area of infectious diseases working with priority populations, including Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples. Specifically, the Senior Project Officer will contribute to the project management and research activities for the implementation and evaluation of new point-of-care testing programs. This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to research that delivers meaningful health outcomes for priority communities. Working within a collaborative and multidisciplinary environment, you will support the planning, implementation and evaluation of innovative testing programs while managing a diverse portfolio of research and implementation activities. The role offers the opportunity to engage with community organisations, health services, government agencies and research partners, helping to drive projects from initiation through to delivery. You will also contribute to research outputs, reports, publications and presentations, supporting evidence generation that informs policy, practice and improved health outcomes. This position is ideally suited to a highly organised professional with strong project management, stakeholder engagement and research support experience who is passionate about making a real-world impact. The role reports to the Senior Lecturer and has no direct reports. Some key skills required: Relevant tertiary qualification in public health, or equivalent through work experience. Masters in Public Health or equivalent is highly desirable. Proven project management experience, with an understanding of the project life cycle and development of project plans, objectives, reporting and documentation. Provide high-quality project management across multiple research and implementation initiatives. Coordinate, plan and complete day-to-day activities within the framework of agreed project timelines and responsibilities. Build and maintain effective stakeholder relationships with community organisations, health services, government agencies, funding bodies, and other key partners at local, jurisdictional, and national levels to support research prioritise and strategic objectives. Proactively assist with research reports, peer-reviewed publications, presentations, ethics applications and progress reports. Excellent stakeholder management with ability to foster strong healthy working relationships with a range of people at different levels, both internally and externally. Experience working with Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander peoples highly desired. Please refer to the position description for full details. UNSW Benefits and Culture 17% Superannuation and leave loading​ Flexible working ​ Additional 3 days of leave over the Christmas Period​ Access to lifelong learning and career development ​ Progressive HR practices​ More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here. In addition to creative a safe and inclusive workplace for our Indigenous staff, we offer other great staff benefits, such as career development opportunities, generous superannuation, additional leave over the end of the year, and other discounts. You can find more information here. To find out more about how we support our Indigenous staff and grow their careers, refer to this page Careers at UNSW for Indigenous Australians | UNSW Sydney To access our Indigenous Strategy, refer to https://www.indigenous.unsw.edu.au/ Pre-Employment Checks: Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks: National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. How to apply: Please click on Apply now to apply online. Applications should not be sent to the contact listed below. Please provide a resume and a copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW . Any questions? For any questions about the kind of support available for our Indigenous staff, get in touch with our Manager, Indigenous Employment, Nat Chenhall: n.chenhall@unsw.edu.au Contact Frank McHenry, Talent Acquisition Associate E: f.mchenry@unsw.edu.au Applications close: Wednesday 9 th September before 11.30pm Sydney time UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 26 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 09 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/540957/lms-platform-engineer-hybrid": {
      "title": "LMS Platform Engineer (Hybrid)",
      "description": "LMS Platform Engineer (Hybrid) Apply now Job no: 540957 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology, Engineer Employment Type: Full-time (35 hours per week) Duration: Fixed-term contract (2 years) until 30th June 2028 Remuneration: $132,445 - $149,228 + 17% super + leave loading Location: UNSW Kensington Campus Hybrid Work Arrangements: Must be available to come to the office 2-3 times a week About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: As an LMS Engineer, you’ll play a critical role in shaping how learning is delivered and experienced across the University. Sitting at the intersection of technology and education, you will design, build, and enhance learning platforms that enable seamless, engaging, and scalable digital learning experiences for staff and students. In this role, your expertise will ensure that LMS solutions are not only technically robust but also aligned to the evolving needs of the University. By leveraging both out-of-the-box capabilities and developing tailored solutions across cloud and on-premise environments, you’ll help deliver flexible, integrated systems that connect with a broader technology ecosystem and support innovation in teaching and learning. Working closely with developers and stakeholders, you’ll guide best-practice solution development using agile and iterative methodologies, driving continuous improvement and high-quality outcomes. Your ability to bridge technical and functional domains will ensure that platforms are reliable, secure, and fit-for-purpose. Ultimately, your work will directly contribute to enhancing UNSW’s digital learning environment—ensuring that systems are efficient, scalable, and capable of supporting world-class education now and into the future. Purpose of the role: Deliver the technical build, configuration, and integration work required to stand up UNSW’s new Learning Management System (LMS) and support the platform and ecosystem commitments through to go-live, noting the new LMS application has not yet been announced. Key responsibilities 1. Platform configuration and build ·         Configure LMS toolsets, features, org structure (faculties, courses, communities), home pages, widgets, and dashboards in line with UNSW requirements . ·         Support role and permissions configuration (scoped roles at system, faculty, course, and assessment level) ·         Configure institutional branding, course templates, and blueprint courses 2. Integrations ·         Assist with the development of integrations between LMS and UNSW systems of record (PeopleSoft/SIS, Entra ID/SSO, Turnitin, Inspera, Leganto, STACK, and other LTI tools) using the LMS vendor Standard CSV, APIs, and LTI Advantage standards ·         Work with the integration ·         Support SSO/identity configuration work delivered through the LMS vendor Professional Services during implementation 3. Data, reporting and analytics ·         Assist with support Data Hub exports, Insights Dashboards, and course-level dashboards for institutional reporting ·         Coordinate with the Data Engineer roles on data flows into UNSW's reporting/BI environment 4. Testing and quality ·         Support configuration testing against acceptance criteria developed with UNSW stakeholders ·         Work with the Test teams on test planning and defect resolution 5. Migration support ·         Support course conversion/migration activity from the incumbent LMS, working with the new LMS’ bulk migration tooling and UNSW's export packages ·         Work with learning designers to remediate migration issues 6. Business case and program delivery support ·         Support stage-gate and governance reporting to the LMS Steering Committee, ITPMO, and Student Systems Reference Group as required For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: Relevant degree or equivalent experience, with strong expertise across multiple technology domains (e.g. web applications, cloud platforms, infrastructure, databases, security). Demonstrated ability to analyse, design, and implement complex technical solutions, applying new technologies and concepts. Strong experience working in Agile delivery environments, with a focus on iterative and high-quality solution outcomes. Proficiency using delivery and workflow management tools (e.g. JIRA, MS TFS) to track and manage work. Experience preparing and executing test cases, with a solid understanding of end-to-end testing practices. Excellent interpersonal and stakeholder engagement skills, with a proven ability to build strong working relationships across IT and the business. Strong time management and organisational skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and meet deadlines. Collaborative and adaptable team player, able to work independently while coaching and supporting others on complex delivery. Strong customer focus, negotiation, and problem-resolution skills, with a commitment to delivering high-quality outcomes. Demonstrated alignment with UNSW values, policies, and health and safety responsibilities. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world.  Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Submit your CV along with a cover letter addressing your suitability for the role via the application portal before 11:30pm on Tuesday, the 8th of September 2026. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW . For queries regarding the position, contact Timothy Hands, the LMS Program Manager, UNSW: timothy.hands@unsw.edu.au For queries regarding the recruitment process, contact Ayla Kamal, Talent Acquisition Partner, UNSW : ayla.kamal@unsw.edu.au *Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal* Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 25 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 08 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/540959/lms-ecosystem-engineer-hybrid": {
      "title": "LMS Ecosystem Engineer (Hybrid)",
      "description": "LMS Ecosystem Engineer (Hybrid) Apply now Job no: 540959 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology, Engineer Employment Type: Full-time (35 hours per week) Duration: Fixed-term contract (2 years) until December 2028 Remuneration: $132,445 - $149,228 + 17% super + leave loading Location: UNSW Kensington Campus Hybrid Work Arrangements : Expectations to come to the office 2-3 times a week About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: As an LMS Engineer, you’ll play a critical role in shaping how learning is delivered and experienced across the University. Sitting at the intersection of technology and education, you will design, build, and enhance learning platforms that enable seamless, engaging, and scalable digital learning experiences for staff and students. In this role, your expertise will ensure that LMS solutions are not only technically robust but also aligned to the evolving needs of the University. By leveraging both out-of-the-box capabilities and developing tailored solutions across cloud and on-premise environments, you’ll help deliver flexible, integrated systems that connect with a broader technology ecosystem and support innovation in teaching and learning. Working closely with developers and stakeholders, you’ll guide best-practice solution development using agile and iterative methodologies, driving continuous improvement and high-quality outcomes. Your ability to bridge technical and functional domains will ensure that platforms are reliable, secure, and fit-for-purpose. Ultimately, your work will directly contribute to enhancing UNSW’s digital learning environment—ensuring that systems are efficient, scalable, and capable of supporting world-class education now and into the future. Purpose of the role: Deliver the technical build, configuration, and integration work required to stand up UNSW’s new Learning Management System (LMS) and support the platform and ecosystem commitments through to go-live, noting the new LMS application has not yet been announced. Key responsibilities 1. Platform configuration and build ·         Configure LMS toolsets, features, org structure (faculties, courses, communities), home pages, widgets, and dashboards in line with UNSW requirements . ·         Support role and permissions configuration (scoped roles at system, faculty, course, and assessment level) ·         Configure institutional branding, course templates, and blueprint courses 2. Integrations ·         Assist with the development of integrations between LMS and UNSW systems of record (PeopleSoft/SIS, Entra ID/SSO, Turnitin, Inspera, Leganto, STACK, and other LTI tools) using the LMS vendor Standard CSV, APIs, and LTI Advantage standards ·         Work with the integration ·         Support SSO/identity configuration work delivered through the LMS vendor Professional Services during implementation 3. Data, reporting and analytics ·         Assist with support Data Hub exports, Insights Dashboards, and course-level dashboards for institutional reporting ·         Coordinate with the Data Engineer roles on data flows into UNSW's reporting/BI environment 4. Testing and quality ·         Support configuration testing against acceptance criteria developed with UNSW stakeholders ·         Work with the Test teams on test planning and defect resolution 5. Migration support ·         Support course conversion/migration activity from the incumbent LMS, working with the new LMS’ bulk migration tooling and UNSW's export packages ·         Work with learning designers to remediate migration issues 6. Business case and program delivery support ·         Support stage-gate and governance reporting to the LMS Steering Committee, ITPMO, and Student Systems Reference Group as required For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: Relevant degree or equivalent experience, with strong expertise across multiple technology domains (e.g. web applications, cloud platforms, infrastructure, databases, security). Demonstrated ability to analyse, design, and implement complex technical solutions, applying new technologies and concepts. Strong experience working in Agile delivery environments, with a focus on iterative and high-quality solution outcomes. Proficiency using delivery and workflow management tools (e.g. JIRA, MS TFS) to track and manage work. Experience preparing and executing test cases, with a solid understanding of end-to-end testing practices. Excellent interpersonal and stakeholder engagement skills, with a proven ability to build strong working relationships across IT and the business. Strong time management and organisational skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and meet deadlines. Collaborative and adaptable team player, able to work independently while coaching and supporting others on complex delivery. Strong customer focus, negotiation, and problem-resolution skills, with a commitment to delivering high-quality outcomes. Demonstrated alignment with UNSW values, policies, and health and safety responsibilities. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world.  Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Submit your CV along with a cover letter addressing your suitability for the role via the application portal before 11:30pm on Tuesday, the 8th of September 2026. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW . For queries regarding the position, contact Timothy Hands, the LMS Program Manager, UNSW: timothy.hands@unsw.edu.au For queries regarding the recruitment process, contact Ayla Kamal, Talent Acquisition Partner, UNSW : ayla.kamal@unsw.edu.au *Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal* Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 25 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 08 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
