    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542175/research-officer-clinical-ai-centre-for-big-data-research-in-health": {
      "title": "Research Officer (Clinical AI)- Centre for Big Data Research in Health",
      "description": "Research Officer (Clinical AI)- Centre for Big Data Research in Health Apply now Job no: 542175 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Research Administration support, Clinical/Medical Research Employment Type: Full Time; 35 hours per week Duration: 2 years fixed term Remuneration: Professional Level 6 $108,021 - $115,233 (based on experience) + 17% super+ annual leave loading Location: Kensington Campus, New South Wales . Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold unrestricted work rights to be considered for this position. Position Summary The Research Officer supports Clinical Artificial Intelligence (AI) research projects within the Centre for Big Data Research in Health, working as part of a multidisciplinary team to advance the use of AI in healthcare. The role provides technical and research support across project design and delivery, with a focus on digital health, electronic medical record (EMR) data, predictive modelling, data analysis, research ethics and data governance. Working closely with senior researchers, NSW Local Health Districts, eHealth NSW and other research partners, the Research Officer will support data management and analysis, research governance and ethics processes, preparation of publications and reports, stakeholder engagement, and the coordination of project activities and milestones. The position reports to the Professor of Clinical AI and has no direct reports. Some key skills required: A university degree in public health, medicine, nursing, digital health, biostatistics or related discipline, or an equivalent level of knowledge gained through a combination of education, training or experience. Experience supporting health, clinical or digital health research projects, including research ethics and governance processes. Experience working with clinical or electronic medical record data/or health datasets would be highly desirable. Experience with data management, quantitative analysis and research project coordination would be desirable. Knowledge of research protocols, experimental design and planning as involved in developing research projects and reporting against milestones. Experience working with a range of computer systems and applications, including data analysis. Please refer to the position description for full details. Benefits and Culture: UNSW offer a competitive salary and access to a plethora of UNSW-perks including: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments 10 days paid cultural leave per year Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here How to Apply: Make each day matter with a meaningful career at UNSW. Submit your application including your resume & cover letter addressing the selection criteria, online before Tuesday 15 th September at 11:55pm. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, WWCC and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Get in Touch: Aarti a.aarti@unsw.edu.au Talent Acquisition Associate Please apply through the application portal and not via the contact above. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 25 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 15 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542254/executive-officer-academic-board": {
      "title": "Executive Officer- Academic Board",
      "description": "Executive Officer- Academic Board Apply now Job no: 542254 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Project Management, Governance, Policy & Compliance Remuneration: Level 9, $153k- 163k+ 17% super + leave loading Full-time (35 hours week) Continuing role Location: Kensington (Hybrid work arrangement available) The Academic Board is the principal academic body of the University of New South Wales, overseeing academic governance and the maintenance of academic standards. As well as supervising academic programs approval, the function has oversight of academic policy, integrity and risk across the university. As Executive Officer, you’ll play an integral role in providing high-level executive and administrative support to the President of the Academic Board, and Academic Board to coordinate critical issues, services and projects. Overseeing the organisation of high-level committees and forums, and develop briefings, speeches and strategic documents, you’ll enable effective oversight and governance for the function. Reporting to the President of the Academic Board, you will work with senior leaders, key stakeholders, and a range of academic and professional staff and students across the University to connect strategy, governance and delivery. This is a trusted role requiring sound judgement, discretion, initiative and a strong commitment to the University’s values and purpose. This role may oversee project staff to support the strategic, operational and administrative aspects of the Academic Board. The Organisation UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. But what makes us different isn’t only what we do, it’s how we do it. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. It’s the reason we’re Australia’s number one university for impact and among the nation’s Top 20 employers. And it’s why we come to campus each day. Who you are Holding a relevant tertiary qualification, you can demonstrate your strategic program and resource planning skills, with a proven track record in project management within the higher education sector. Your communication skills and attention to detail ensure high-quality outputs including reports, documentation and presentations. Comfortable operating in a fast-paced and complex environment, you exercise sound judgement and tact while managing competing priorities. Your strong analytical skills enable you to address complex business challenges and provide informed advice. Experience in working with governance processes in large organisations, together with exceptional relationship-building, influencing, and negotiation skills, will enable you to collaborate effectively with stakeholders across the University and contribute to the achievement of departmental objectives. For further details on the role responsibility and skills & experience required, please refer to the position description To apply :  If leveraging your advanced interpersonal skills to anticipate the needs of the Academic Board to provide executive-level operational support is of interest to you, please submit your CV, Cover Letter and responses to Skills and Experience required via the APPLY button by Monday 7 September 2026, 11:55pm. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Additional 3 days leave during December shutdown period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Job queries contact: For queries on the recruitment process, please contact Katherine Molloy: katherine.molloy@unsw.edu.au Please note applications sent directly to the contacts above cannot be accepted. Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 25 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 07 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542290/senior-service-designer": {
      "title": "Senior Service Designer",
      "description": "Senior Service Designer Apply now Job no: 542290 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology Employment Type: Full Time, Fixed Term 2-year position Remuneration: Level 9: From $153,933 + 17% superannuation and leave loading Location: Kensington with transition to newly renovated office at our Randwick campus, anticipated for October 2026 About UNSW At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work.  We aspire to be Australia’s global university, improving and transforming lives through excellence in research, outstanding education and a commitment to advancing a just society. About the Role The Business Optimisation Strategy (BOS) is a landmark investment to modernise UNSW’s enterprise platforms and transform service delivery across HR, Finance and IT through the implementation of the ServiceNow and Oracle SaaS platforms. By simplifying processes, improving data foundations, and enabling intelligent automation, BOS supports UNSW’s Progress for All strategy — returning valuable time to staff for teaching, research, and student impact. As BOS mobilises, we are seeking an experienced Senior Service Designer (titled internally as Design Operations Lead) to optimise UNSW's design processes, workflows, and systems to deliver high-impact, human-centered solutions at scale. Reporting to the Service Transformation Program Manager with no direct reports, the role provides strategic leadership to align operations with University and Faculty priorities. Key responsibilities include driving operational excellence, human centred design, building organisational capability, and guiding strategic decision-making across the institution. Responsibilities Align BOS Service design with broader organisational design and experience initiatives. Lead BOS Service Design and refine services and workflows to improve design efficiency, quality, experience and scalability. Define and standardise experience principles that adhere to good HCD, accessibility and service design practises. Track, delivery progress and key performance metrics and report strategic insights to senior stakeholders to guide decision-making. Drive Human-Centred Design capability to influence design decisions to deliver a coherent, accessible and good end to end customer experience.. Foster cross-functional collaboration and consultation to embed best practices and continuous improvement. About You Relevant tertiary qualifications paired with extensive expertise in end-to-end improvement and transformation. Proven track record leading design operations and service design initiatives in complex environments. Strong leadership and stakeholder engagement skills, with experience managing multidisciplinary teams and advising senior executives. Advanced capability in design systems, customer journey mapping, and experience design. High-level communication and facilitation skills, with a focus on driving change, allocating resources, and balancing competing priorities. Thorough understanding of UNSW values, strategic aims, and institutional health and safety responsibilities. For further information on the role & responsibilities, please refer to the Position Description How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter detailing your interest and suitability for the job (as per the skills & experience bullet points in the Position Description ) before Wednesday 9 September by 11:55pm. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Additional 3 days leave during December festive period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Position Description Advertised: 25 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 09 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542294/academic-offerings-consultant": {
      "title": "Academic Offerings Consultant",
      "description": "Academic Offerings Consultant Apply now Job no: 542294 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Governance, Policy & Compliance Employment Type: Full Time (35 hours per week) Duration: Fixed Term Contract until October 2027 Remuneration: Level 7 from $118k + 17% superannuation & leave loading Location:  Kensington NSW (Hybrid Working Arrangements) Why your role matters The Academic Offerings Consultant works as part of a team that is responsible for the effective operational oversight, development, and ongoing support of academic offering processes, policies, systems, and services within the Division of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Academic Quality). Positioned within the Academic Offerings Team, the role reports to the Manager, Academic Offerings and does not have any direct reports. Specific accountabilities include: Review academic proposals within the curriculum management system and provide detailed expert advice to a range of stakeholders through interpretation of policy, as well as editorial advice, and support with systems, workflows, and approvals. Work with the team to develop and maintain systems, procedures, and guidelines to ensure a consistent approach to academic administration business processes. Support compliance with relevant policies, procedures, and legislation. Deliver effective operational management and support of UNSW’s academic offerings governance, record keeping, and publishing services and systems. About You: Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Excellent time management and organisational skills, and the ability to work well under pressure and manage multiple tasks with competing deadlines. Demonstrated ability to influence and guide staff. Highly advanced interpersonal, written and oral communication skills. Demonstrated experience providing effective customer service and support to clients at all levels. Highly proficient computer literacy with excellent skills in Microsoft Office applications Experience with curriculum management and student systems would be beneficial. Ability to plan, organise and co-ordinate projects and tasks. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. For further details on role responsibilities and skills & experience required, refer to the position description found under the position listing on Jobs@UNSW . Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible working options. Career development opportunities. 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments. Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness). For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. How to Apply: Submit your CV & Cover Letter addressing your suitability to the role in relation to the selection criteria (in approximately 2 pages) before 11.55pm Monday 7 th September 2026. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. Job queries contact: Chloe Gane, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: chloe.gane@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 25 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 07 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542309/manager-language-services": {
      "title": "Manager Language Services",
      "description": "Manager Language Services Apply now Job no: 542309 Work type: Full Time Location: Canberra, ACT Categories: Project Management, Management Manager Language Services, UNSW Canberra Employment type: Full-time, on-site 5 days per week, 35 hours Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Level 8 from $132,445 + leave loading + 17% super Location : Barton, Canberra Work rights required: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates hold Australian Citizenship and be able to obtain and maintain security clearance to be considered for this position. About UNSW Canberra University of New South Wales (UNSW) in Canberra has multiple locations in the Nation’s Capital. UNSW Canberra distinguishes itself from other institutions by its commitment to being thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all endeavours. This combined approach is integral to the university's impact and contributes to its recognition as one of the top 20 universities globally, as well as a proud member of Australia's esteemed Group of Eight. Choosing a career at UNSW means embracing an environment where thriving, facing challenges, and engaging in meaningful work are not just encouraged but integral to the university experience. If you seek a career where you can excel and contribute meaningfully, you've found the right place. At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. Why Your Role Matters: Working in a fast-paced and dynamic environment, the Manager will be responsible for the successful delivery of a large-scale high-value education contract with a government department. The Manager will monitor performance and lead a large pool of casual language teachers to deliver educational services to a high standard. The Manager will promote and manage productive relationships with University and Government colleagues, visitors and students. The Manager reports to Herad of Innovation & Industry Partnerships and has 4 direct reports and a large cohort of casual staff to supervise. Accountabilities: • Oversee the successful implementation and maintenance of the University’s delivery of the contract services in accordance with best practice, within agreed timeframes, and at the standards required by the Government client. • Provide effective leadership, direction, and support to the administration team and pool of casual staff to achieve goals and ensure the continued improvement of services for learners and the Government client. • Engage in and support activities, including preparing high-level responses for government requests for quotes, and nurture relationships with a broad range of Commonwealth agencies. • Oversee the extensive production and review of performance, budget, project objective and other internal and external reporting associated with the provision of services to the Government client. • Initiate, advise and implement management procedures that support and maintain networks, links and relationships, while providing exceptional sustainable stakeholder engagement and communication skills, overseeing the Government client relations. • Take a leading operational role in providing influence over the establishment of priorities, programs and/or budgets (formulation and expenditure) for a major specialised project with scope to reset priorities or resources within overall program objectives. • Apply a thorough knowledge of University-wide policies, the external environment (e.g. government legislation, guidelines and requirements), diverse research and teaching activities. Who You Are (skills and experience): • Tertiary qualifications with relevant experience, or an equivalent level of knowledge gained through any other combination of education, training and/or experience. • Evidence of success in establishing, cultivating and maintaining high level relationships, with the ability to influence key internal and external stakeholders. • Demonstrated ability to work in an agile, fast paced environment with competing demands and deadlines. • Excellent ability to manage and schedule a large number of employees to deliver services to an external supplier. • Demonstrated success in project management and leadership. • Excellent organisational and time management skills with the ability to work on several projects in an efficient manner. Benefits and Culture: UNSW is committed to helping staff balance work-life responsibilities, by providing access to high-quality services, facilities, and flexible work and leave arrangements. Additional three days leave during December festive period Subsidised under-building secure parking available Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice Free gym access UNSW-wide strategy to focus on Healthy Body, Healthy Mind, Healthy Places and Healthy Culture. You can learn more about our Benefits and Culture at Lifestyle & Benefits UNSW Eligibility: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must be Australian Citizens to be considered for this position. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. How to Apply: Make each day matter with a meaningful career at UNSW. Submit your application online before Monday 7 September 2026 at 11:30pm , including your CV and a 1–2-page cover letter addressing the required skills and experience outlined in the position description. In order to view the position description, please ensure that you allow pop-ups for https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au Get in Touch: For role-related queries please contact: Davina Mansfield - Head of Innovation & Industry Partnerships d.mansfield@unsw.edu.au For recruitment process-related queries please contact: Holly Whale – Talent Acquisition Partner h.whale@unsw.edu.au Applications cannot be accepted if sent directly to the contact listed. Find out more about the lifestyle and benefits when working with UNSW For further information about UNSW Canberra, please visit: UNSW Canberra For further information on living in Canberra, please visit: Living in Canberra UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 25 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 07 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542467/program-administrator-school-of-aviation": {
      "title": "Program Administrator - School of Aviation",
      "description": "Program Administrator - School of Aviation Apply now Job no: 542467 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration, Student Services, Student Recruitment / Admissions $92K - $115K (broadband level 5/6) + 17% Superannuation Fixed term Full-time contract until March 2028 UNSW Kensington Campus (hybrid) The Faculty of Science teaches approximately 7000 full-time student equivalents, with an academic staff of approximately 600 and general staff of 300. The Faculty is one of the largest and most complex faculties of UNSW Sydney. It comprises the Schools of Aviation, Biological Earth and Environmental Science (BEES), Biotechnology and Biomolecular Sciences (BABS), Chemistry, Materials Science and Engineering (MSE), Mathematics and Statistics, Psychology, and Physics. It also includes a number of major Research Centres and field stations. About the role Embedded in the School of Aviation, the Program Administrator will drive the continuous improvement cycle for student services within the School, and will oversee the functions for enquiries management, student communications and placements and business process improvement. The Program Administrator is responsible for providing administrative support to the School for the smooth running of degree programs. The role oversees program level administrative activities that assist students enrolled in one or more programs of study. The role is also responsible for providing strong administrative teaching support to stakeholders to ensure that the School’s teaching function is delivered efficiently as required. The role of Program Administrator reports to the School Manager and has no direct reports. This broadbanded role may be appointed at Level 5 or Level 6, depending on the scope of responsibilities and the successful candidate’s experience. Level 5 responsibilities: Provide administrative support for Faculty degree programs, including admissions, scheduling, enrolment, assessment, exams, student progression and Moodle course setup. Monitor class utilisation and forecast enrolments to support School planning. Maintain accurate academic, student and marketing information across relevant systems, websites and publications. Support technology-enabled learning and teaching activities, including Learning Management System administration. Act as a School triage and Tier 3 escalation point, resolving complex student matters and providing high-quality service to students and staff. Provide individual student support, keep accurate case notes and ensure consistent, high-quality advice. Contribute to program administration, process improvement, student prizes and awards events. In addition to the Level 5 responsibilities, the Level 6 role will also: Apply specialist knowledge across multiple academic programs. Advise Faculty and Divisional leaders on student and teaching support priorities. Coordinate complex student cohort activities and contribute to cross-Faculty business improvement projects. About you (selection criteria) To succeed in this role, you will bring the following skills and experience. Level 5: Experience in the higher education sector, with an understanding of student and academic services gained through admissions, student services, teaching support or a similar function. Experience supporting students across the student lifecycle, from application through progression, as well as providing teaching support (term planning etc). Strong digital capability, including Microsoft applications, learning management systems and database management. Strong organisational skills, with the ability to prioritise, manage complex tasks and projects, meet competing deadlines and maintain accuracy. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build effective relationships with a wide range of internal and external stakeholders. Ability to analyse and resolve sensitive issues efficiently and effectively. Ability to interpret, apply and improve policies, systems, manuals, rules, procedures and guidelines. Proven ability to work collaboratively while taking initiative and ownership of your own work. For a Level 6, in addition to the Level 5 criteria, you will also have: Significant relevant experience in a similar role within a tertiary organisation. Demonstrated ability to apply knowledge and significant prior experience to resolve complex and sensitive issues. Substantial customer service experience, including providing quality advice to stakeholders on complex and sensitive matters. This a parental leave cover approved until March 2028. We are looking for someone to start at the end of October at the latest (to allow appropriate handover). There are no sponsorship available for this position. Application documents: please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your motivation for applying and addressing the “About You” section of this job ad. For informal queries, please see the below contact details. Otherwise, please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent directly to the contact listed. Contact: Anne de Voronine, Talent Acquisition Associate E: a.de_voronine@unsw.edu.au Applications close: 8 th September 11.55PM ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au Position Description Advertised: 25 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 08 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542486/teaching-support-officer-medicine-health": {
      "title": "Teaching Support Officer- Medicine & Health",
      "description": "Teaching Support Officer- Medicine & Health Apply now Job no: 542486 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration, Student Services Employment Type: Full Time; 35 hours per week Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Professional Broadband Level 5/6 $92,265 - $108,021 (based on experience) + 17% super+ annual leave loading Location: Kensington Campus, New South Wales . Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold unrestricted work rights to be considered for this position. Position Summary The Teaching Support Officer provides day-to-day operational and administrative support for postgraduate coursework teaching across UNSW Medicine & Health. The role supports the effective delivery of teaching activities across the academic lifecycle, including term planning, timetabling, class management, learning management systems, assessments and examinations, results processing, enrolments, academic casual engagement and the coordination of teaching resources and materials. At Level 5 , the role provides high-quality service to students, academics and other stakeholders, coordinating teaching support activities in accordance with University policies and processes and contributing to continuous improvement across the Faculty. At Level 6 , the role takes on greater responsibility for the oversight and coordination of complex teaching support processes, provides advice on relevant policies and procedures, works across multiple teams and administrative units, and identifies and implements opportunities to improve and streamline service delivery. Some key skills required: At Level 5: Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively and productively within a team, but also to take initiative and work independently while managing competing demands. Demonstrated experience providing effective customer service and support to clients at all levels. Well-developed interpersonal and written and verbal communication skills. Advanced time management, organisational and problem-solving skills, including the ability to meet deadlines. High proficiency of computer literacy, working with a range of computer systems and applications, including Microsoft Office 365 suite including Teams, OneDrive, Excel, Word, Powerpoint and Outlook and with using UNSW’s enterprise systems. Demonstrated experience supporting and contributing to projects and initiatives. Level 6 (in addition to Level 5 above): Ability to work independently with minimal supervision and work productively as part of a team. Significant relevant experience in a similar role in a tertiary organisation. High level ability to apply knowledge and draw on significant experience to analyse and resolve complex and sensitive issues efficiently and effectively. Substantial experience in providing a broad range of teaching administration support and specialist advice to Academic staff. Please refer to the position description for full details. Benefits and Culture: UNSW offer a competitive salary and access to a plethora of UNSW-perks including: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments 10 days paid cultural leave per year Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here How to Apply: Make each day matter with a meaningful career at UNSW. Submit your application including your resume & cover letter addressing the selection criteria, online before Sunday 6 th September at 11:55pm. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, WWCC and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Get in Touch: Aarti a.aarti@unsw.edu.au Talent Acquisition Associate Please apply through the application portal and not via the contact above. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 25 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 06 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542522/senior-administrative-officer": {
      "title": "Senior Administrative Officer",
      "description": "Senior Administrative Officer Apply now Job no: 542522 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration Employment Type: Full Time (35 hours per week) Duration: Fixed Term until March 2028 Remuneration: Level 7 from $118k + 17% superannuation and leave loading Location:  Kensington NSW (Hybrid Working Arrangements) Why your role matters The Senior Administrative Officer provides high-level administrative and operational support to the Division of Education and Student Experience, contributing to strategic initiatives, continuous improvement and the delivery of high-quality services to students and staff. The role requires excellent stakeholder management skills and the ability to build trusted relationships with stakeholders at all levels. Reporting to the Operations Manager, the position supports the effective day-to-day functioning of the divisional office. Specific accountabilities include: Work with the Director of Operations and Divisional Office team to coordinate general administration across Division of Education and Student Experience delivering an efficient support service. Manage and direct workflows and governance requirements within the administrative team, providing guidance and support across the broader Divisional Operational teams. Develop, implement and maintain systems and procedures to ensure a consistent approach for general administration and support, including document and records management, to ensure compliance with relevant policies and procedures. Provide expert advice and technical knowledge regarding administrative services to key stakeholders. About You: Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Excellent time management and organisational skills, and the ability to work well under pressure and manage multiple tasks with competing deadlines. Demonstrated ability to supervise, influence and guide staff. Highly advanced interpersonal, written and oral communication skills. Demonstrated experience providing effective customer service and support to clients at all levels. Highly proficient computer literacy with excellent skills in Microsoft Office applications. Ability to plan, organise and co-ordinate projects and tasks. Excellent numeracy skills with the ability to understand and communicate financial information and monitor budgets. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. For further details on role responsibilities and skills & experience required, refer to the position description found under the position listing on Jobs@UNSW . Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible working options. Career development opportunities. 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments. Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness). For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter addressing the key selection criteria (no more than 2 pages) before 11.55pm Sunday 6th September 2026. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. Job queries contact: Chloe Gane, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: chloe.gane@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 24 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 06 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542661/international-partnerships-coordinator": {
      "title": "International Partnerships Coordinator",
      "description": "International Partnerships Coordinator Apply now Job no: 542661 Work type: Part Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration, Marketing / Communications, Project Management International Partnerships Coordinator Employment Type: Part Time (21 hours per week) Duration: Fixed Term Contract until September 2028 Remuneration: Level 6 from $108,021 - $115,233 depending on experience (pro rata) + 17% super + leave loading Location: UNSW Kensington Campus (Hybrid) About UNSW: UNSW isn't like other places you've worked. Yes, we're a large organisation with a diverse and talented community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical and purposeful in everything we do. As Australia's #1 university and one of the world's leading research institutions, our people are helping solve society's biggest challenges through world-class research, education and innovation. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and make a genuine impact, you're in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: As International Partnerships Coordinator, you will support the Faculty of Law & Justice’s international engagement priorities and help turn strategic initiatives into well-coordinated activities, programs and partnerships. Working closely with the Associate Dean International, you will provide high-level administrative, operational and project support across international agreements, visits, student mobility, funding and stakeholder engagement. The role reports to the Associate Dean International, with a dotted reporting line to the Faculty Operations Manager, and has no direct reports. Key Responsibilities: Coordinate and support international engagement initiatives, marketing opportunities and student-related activities. Build and maintain effective relationships with internal and external stakeholders, including the International Portfolio, to support the Faculty’s international objectives. Prepare reports, briefings, presentations and other materials for strategic activities, international visits and delegations. Coordinate travel arrangements and provide operational support to the Associate Dean International, using sound judgement to manage competing priorities. Administer international agreements and Memoranda of Understanding, ensuring compliance with University policies and procedures. Support Faculty-led international programs, competitions and partnership activities. Manage, monitor and report on the Law & Justice international budget. Work with relevant teams and students to administer mobility grants, international awards and scholarship funding in line with applicable guidelines and timelines. Maintain effective communication with stakeholders, analyse requests, filter correspondence and prioritise urgent matters on behalf of the Associate Dean International. Who You Are: You are a highly organised and collaborative professional who communicates clearly, works confidently with a wide range of stakeholders and brings sound judgement to a varied workload. You can work independently when needed while contributing productively as part of a team. A relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with strong attention to detail. Demonstrated success working collaboratively on initiatives with people at different levels of an organisation. Advanced computer literacy, including strong skills across Microsoft Office applications. Excellent time management skills and the ability to manage multiple tasks, changing priorities and competing deadlines. The initiative and judgement to work independently, solve problems and respond effectively to competing demands. For further details on role responsibilities and skills & experience required, refer to the position description found under the position listing on Jobs@UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. It's our people who make UNSW one of the world's leading universities. Along with the opportunity to work on diverse, high-risk projects that support world-class research and education, you'll enjoy benefits that are hard to find in many construction and infrastructure environments, including: 17% superannuation and additional leave loading. Additional 3 days' leave over the Christmas shutdown. Flexible working options and genuine work-life balance. Career development and learning opportunities. Discounts and entitlements across retail, education and fitness. To learn more about working at UNSW, visit: https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits How to Apply: Submit your CV & Cover Letter by Monday 7 th September 2026 Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. Contact: Amy Denney a.denney@unsw.edu.au - Talent Acquisition Partner Please apply directly through the website , applications sent to the contact above will not be accepted. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role . UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 24 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 07 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/534544/principal-incident-response-analyst": {
      "title": "Principal Incident Response Analyst",
      "description": "Principal Incident Response Analyst Apply now Job no: 534544 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology, Analyst, Cyber Full time continuing role as Principal Incident Response Analyst Excellent salary package available Kensington, Sydney location, 2-3 days in the office, Hybrid working About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. It’s the reason we’re one of the top 20 universities in the world (QS top 20) and a member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. With a significant uplift across our Cyber teams, the Principal Incident Response Analyst will serve as a senior technical authority within the UNSW CSIRT team, providing expertise across incident response, digital forensics, threat intelligence and threat hunting. The role will lead response to complex and high-impact cyber security incidents following recognised frameworks and methodologies, while working closely with internal stakeholders and external partners, including MSSPs. The role reports to the Manager, Cyber Security Incident Response and has no direct reports. Specific accountabilities for this role include: Serve as the primary technical contact for cyber security incidents impacting UNSW, coordinating response activities with operational teams, and keeping key stakeholders informed of critical developments. Conduct and lead advanced technical investigations into complex security incidents, utilising in depth expertise in digital forensics, malware analysis, and network analysis to identify root causes, trace threat actor activities, and contain threats. Provide expert guidance and technical oversight to the CSIRT team in delivering advanced incident response, threat hunting, threat intelligence, and digital forensics services. Drive the development, enhancement, and continuous refinement of incident response processes, ensuring they are robust, technically precise, and aligned with industry best practices. Leverage AI, machine learning, and cutting-edge technologies to enhance our capabilities. Facilitate technical collaboration between internal UNSW teams and external MSSP partners, ensuring the alignment of threat detection, analysis, and response activities to maintain an integrated and efficient incident response capability. Document and report on critical incidents, producing in-depth technical post-incident review (PIR) reports that detail the root cause, attack vectors, impacted infrastructure, and actions taken. Ensure reports align with established processes, standards, and frameworks, providing comprehensive insights and recommendations. Prepare and deliver strategic security reports, analysing trends, patterns, and insights to provide actionable recommendations that strengthen the organisation’s security posture, and sharing findings with relevant stakeholders Proactively analyse and apply cyber threat intelligence to strengthen organisational defences, supporting mitigation of cyber threats targeting the organisation. Lead the generation and refinement of actionable threat intelligence, analysing data from internal and external sources to identify emerging threats, attack trends, and adversary behaviours. Integrate intelligence into playbooks, operating procedures, and the knowledge base to enhance our security capabilities. Conduct proactive threat hunting to detect malicious actors and anomalies, leveraging advanced analytical techniques to uncover potential security threats in real time. Contribute to the development, implementation, and optimisation of security controls, services, and technologies to mitigate risks working closely with the Security Engineering team Adhere to IT Service Management practices across UNSW IT, Faculties, Divisions, and Affiliates Oversee and finalise effective communications with key stakeholders, both internal and external and provide influential input with stakeholders to achieve business outcomes. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values Ensure hazards and risks psychosocial and physical are identified and controlled for tasks, projects, and activities that pose a health and safety risk within your area of responsibility. Who you are: A relevant tertiary qualification with extensive experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training, and experience. Minimum ten years of industry experience in cyber security incident response, with a minimum five years of management experience in a cyber security environment or in a senior support role. Strong technical skills and operational experience in the management, configuration, and support of security controls including network security, endpoint security, application security, data protection, and/or identity and access management. Comprehensive analytical and problem-solving skills and proven capacity to exercise initiative, flexibility and to be proactive in the development of robust solutions to problems. Strong consultation, influencing and negotiation skills and proven ability to engage effectively with diverse stakeholders to achieve successful outcomes. Strong written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail for deliverables produced. Demonstrated success working effectively and collaboratively on initiatives with a range of people at different levels within an organisation. Excellent time management skills, with a demonstrated ability to respond to changing priorities, manage multiple tasks and meet competing deadlines by using judgement and initiative. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Relevant industry certifications such as GCFA, GREM, OSCP and CCSP are highly desirable but not required. Benefits and Culture Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits To apply:  Please submit your CV, Cover Letter and responses addressing the required Who you are criteria. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role; valid Australian working rights are required on application. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact: Jen MacLachlan - Talent Acquisition Partner. e: j.maclachlan@unsw.edu.au Please apply through the application portal as we do not accept direct applications to the email above. Applications close: Sunday 13th of September at 11.30pm UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 21 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 13 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542194/manager-cyber-security-incident-response": {
      "title": "Manager, Cyber Security Incident Response",
      "description": "Manager, Cyber Security Incident Response Apply now Job no: 542194 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology, Cyber Full time continuing role as Manager, Cyber Security Incident Response Excellent salary package available Kensington, Sydney location, 2-3 days in the office, Hybrid working About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. It’s the reason we’re one of the top 20 universities in the world (QS top 20) and a member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. With a significant uplift within our Cyber teams, this role of Manager, Cyber Security Incident Response will lead an experienced CSIRT team responsible for managing all aspects of the incident response process operating within a hybrid operating model working closely together with our Managed Security Service Providers (MSSP). The position requires expert knowledge and significant experience in: Cyber security incident management and response, digital forensics, threat intelligence and threat hunting, cyber security frameworks, security technologies and automation including SIEM, EDR and SOAR, and Security Operations Centre service delivery. We need someone who has exceptional interpersonal skills, enabling effective communication and collaboration with partners, vendors, and both internal and external stakeholders. The role will also develop and maintain incident reports, playbooks, procedures, escalation processes, and response plans aligned to best practices. The role reports directly to the Head of Cyber Security Operations. There will be a team of Specialists reporting to the Manager, Cyber Security Incident Response. Specific accountabilities for this role include: Lead and mentor a team of incident response specialists, overseeing major incident response, threat hunting, threat intelligence, and digital forensics services within a hybrid operating model. Foster team development and collaboration ensuring high standards of performance in delivering these critical services. Manage investigations and ensure resources are appropriately assigned to perform actions in partnership with all relevant stakeholders Manage and co-ordinate incident response activities between UNSW and external partners. Act as an escalation point for major security incidents impacting the organisation, coordinating communications and response activities together with other operations teams. Actively participate in crisis management efforts, ensuring alignment between incident response, business continuity, and executive leadership to minimise impact and expedite recovery. Accurately document all actions taken during an incident, preparing detailed technical reports and executive summaries in alignment with established processes, standards, and frameworks. Present security reports on a regular basis analysing trends, patterns, and insights to provide recommendations Provide regular updates on all aspects of the incident management function to ensure issues, risks, and problems are addressed in a timely manner and services are operating effectively following a continuous improvement model Develop and maintain playbooks, operating procedures, technical standards, processes, knowledge base articles and other documentation as required Lead the design, implementation, and continuous improvement of an Agentic SOC operating model, ensuring automation and AI-enabled workflows materially strengthen detection and response capability, while embedding appropriate human oversight, governance, accountability, and operational assurance Analyse and report on cyber threat intelligence to prepare, prevent, and identify threats targeting the organisation Proactively conduct threat hunting to detect anomalous activity leveraging advanced techniques to identify emerging threats, vulnerabilities, and suspicious behaviours across the environment Regularly track and validate supplier performance in accordance with KPI’s, objectives, and contract terms, and resolving or escalating performance issues or disputes Contribute to the development, implementation, and optimisation of security controls, services, and technologies across Security Operations Adhere to IT Service Management practices across UNSW IT, Faculties, Divisions, and Affiliates Oversee and finalise effective communications with key stakeholders, both internal and external and provide influential input with stakeholders to achieve business outcomes. You will be required to be available on-call roster for emergencies. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the psychosocial or physical health and safety of yourself or others. Who you are: A relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training, and experience. Minimum ten years of industry experience in cyber security incident response, with a minimum three years of management experience in a cyber security environment or in a senior support role. Strong technical skills and operational experience in the management, configuration, and support of security controls including network security, endpoint security, application security, data protection, and/or identity and access management. Comprehensive analytical and problem-solving skills and proven capacity to exercise initiative, flexibility and to be proactive in development of robust solutions to problems. Strong consultation, influencing and negotiation skills and proven ability to engage effectively with diverse stakeholders to achieve successful outcomes. Strong written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail for deliverables produced. Demonstrated success working effectively and collaboratively on initiatives with a range of people at different levels within an organisation. Excellent time management skills, with a demonstrated ability to respond to changing priorities, manage multiple tasks and meet competing deadlines by using judgement and initiative. Relevant industry certifications such as CISSP, CISM, GCIH, GCFA are highly desirable but not required. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Benefits and Culture Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc), Career development opportunities, 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments , Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period, Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits To apply:  Please submit your CV, Cover Letter and responses addressing the required Who you are criteria. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role; valid Australian working rights are required on application. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact: Jen MacLachlan - Talent Acquisition Partner- e: j.maclachlan@unsw.edu.au Please apply through the application portal as we do not accept direct applications to the email above. Applications close: Sunday 13th of September at 11.30pm UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 21 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 13 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542461/admissions-team-leader": {
      "title": "Admissions Team Leader",
      "description": "Admissions Team Leader Apply now Job no: 542461 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Student Recruitment / Admissions Duration: 2 Fixed term roles - 1 X Fixed term until end July 2027 and 1 X Fixed term until end Sept 2027 Remuneration: $118,483 + 17% super (level 7) Location: UNSW Kensington Campus (Hybrid Flexible Working) Why Your Role Matters As an Admissions Team Leader, you’ll oversee the day-to-day operations of application and admissions processing across several UNSW faculties. You’ll lead, develop and support a team of up to eight Admissions Officers, ensuring high quality service, accurate assessments and smooth workflows throughout the admissions cycle. This role reports to the Operations Manager. We have two fixed term opportunities, both replacing current employees: one until the end of July 2027 and the other until the end of September 2027. Main Responsibilities (Summary) Lead, coach and develop a team of Admissions Officers, managing daily and peak-period workload within an environment that promotes respect, accountability, quality service and facilities, and fosters initiative and innovation. Ensure fair, consistent and transparent admissions decisions in line with University policy and external regulations. Guide staff to meet service level agreements for application processing and enquiries Assess applicant eligibility and oversee accurate, complete and up-to-date records Collaborate with faculties, Scholarships and other stakeholders to support smooth admissions operations Train staff, improve processes, and maintain clear documentation Manage escalated cases, fostering a culture of customer service excellence and ensure teams place the needs of potential students at the forefront. Contribute to the statistical reports and suggest continuous business improvements activities of the Admissions Office. Who You Are You bring strong leadership, excellent communication skills and a passion for delivering high quality service. You’ll also have: Demonstrated extensive experience in admissions Demonstrated ability to train, motivate and influence teams – previous leadership experience is highly regarded Experience working with diverse stakeholders across cultures Strong customer service focus with a proactive, solutions-oriented mindset High attention to detail and experience managing high volume workflows Advanced digital skills, including Microsoft suite, CRM and student management systems A positive, adaptable approach to change in a dynamic environment Candidates will need to have valid working rights – this role is not available for sponsorship. The role is hybrid – Monday to Friday. We’d expect the successful candidate to work over the end of the year shutdown. Application process: please submit your résumé and a cover letter bringing your experience and motivation to life. Applications close: 8 September 11.55PM Get in Touch (for job related queries only – applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed): Anne de Voronine, Talent Acquisition Associate, a.de_voronine@unsw.edu.au ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 21 Aug 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 08 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
